window.PRELOADED_CHAPTERS = {
  "1": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch1\" data-book=\"history\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">1</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">History: India and the Contemporary World – II</div>\n      <h2>The Rise of Nationalism in Europe</h2>\n      <p>Unit 1: History • French Revolution, Liberalism, Unifications & Imperialism — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s1q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s1q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Write a note on: (a) Giuseppe Mazzini (b) Count Camillo de Cavour (c) The Greek war of independence (d) Frankfurt Parliament (e) The role of women in nationalist struggles.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>(a) Giuseppe Mazzini:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Italian Revolutionary:</strong> Born in Genoa in 1807, Mazzini became a member of the secret society of the Carbonari. At the age of 24, he was sent into exile in 1831 for attempting a revolution in Liguria.</li>\n  <li><strong>Founding Secret Societies:</strong> He subsequently founded two underground societies: <em>Young Italy</em> in Marseilles and <em>Young Europe</em> in Berne, whose members were like-minded young men from Poland, France, Italy, and the German states.</li>\n  <li><strong>Vision of Unified Republic:</strong> Mazzini believed that God had intended nations to be the natural units of mankind. Therefore, Italy could not remain a patchwork of small states and kingdoms; it had to be forged into a single unified republic within a wider alliance of nations. Metternich described him as <em>'the most dangerous enemy of our social order'</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(b) Count Camillo de Cavour:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Chief Minister of Sardinia-Piedmont:</strong> Cavour led the movement to unify the regions of Italy. He was neither a revolutionary nor a democrat. Like many other wealthy and educated members of the Italian elite, he spoke French much better than Italian.</li>\n  <li><strong>Diplomatic Alliance:</strong> Through a tactful diplomatic alliance with France engineered by Cavour, Sardinia-Piedmont succeeded in defeating the Austrian forces in 1859.</li>\n  <li><strong>Architect of Italian Unification:</strong> Combined regular troops with armed volunteers led by Giuseppe Garibaldi, liberating the kingdom of the Two Sicilies in 1860, crowning Victor Emmanuel II king of united Italy in 1861.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(c) The Greek War of Independence:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Mobilisation of Nationalist Feelings:</strong> Greece had been part of the Ottoman Empire since the fifteenth century. Revolutionary nationalism sparked off a struggle for independence amongst the Greeks in 1821.</li>\n  <li><strong>Support from Diaspora and Western Europe:</strong> Nationalists received support from exiled Greeks and West Europeans sympathetic to ancient Greek culture. Poets and artists lauded Greece as the <em>'cradle of European civilisation'</em> (e.g., Lord Byron organized funds and fought in Greece).</li>\n  <li><strong>Treaty of Constantinople (1832):</strong> Finally, the Treaty of Constantinople of 1832 recognized Greece as an independent sovereign nation.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(d) Frankfurt Parliament:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>All-German National Assembly:</strong> In 1848, middle-class professionals, businessmen, and prosperous artisans came together in Frankfurt and voted for an all-German National Assembly.</li>\n  <li><strong>Convening at St. Paul's Church:</strong> On 18 May 1848, 831 elected representatives drafted a constitution for a German nation headed by a constitutional monarchy.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rejection and Dissolution:</strong> Friedrich Wilhelm IV, King of Prussia, rejected the crown and joined other monarchs to oppose the elected assembly. The parliament was disbanded by Prussian troops.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(e) The Role of Women in Nationalist Struggles:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Active Mass Participation:</strong> Women formed their own political associations, founded newspapers, and took part in political meetings and demonstrations.</li>\n  <li><strong>Denial of Suffrage Rights:</strong> Despite their active participation, they were denied voting rights and were admitted only as observers to stand in the visitors’ gallery at the Frankfurt parliament.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mazzini's ideological vision & secret societies</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Cavour's diplomatic maneuvers & unification role</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Greek struggle & Treaty of Constantinople (1832)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Frankfurt Parliament convening & monarchical resistance</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Women's political contributions & denial of rights</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s1q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s1q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What steps did the French revolutionaries take to create a sense of collective identity among the French people?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The French revolutionaries introduced various measures and practices that created a sense of collective identity among the French people:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Ideals of La Patrie and Le Citoyen:</strong> The ideas of <em>la patrie</em> (the fatherland) and <em>le citoyen</em> (the citizen) emphasized the notion of a united community enjoying equal rights under a constitution.</li>\n  <li><strong>New National Symbols:</strong> A new French tricolour flag replaced the former royal standard.</li>\n  <li><strong>Elected National Assembly:</strong> The Estates General was elected by active citizens and renamed the National Assembly.</li>\n  <li><strong>Hymns, Oaths and Commemoration:</strong> New hymns were composed (such as the <em>Marseillaise</em>), oaths taken, and martyrs commemorated, all in the name of the nation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Centralised Administrative System:</strong> A centralized administrative system formulated uniform laws for all citizens within its territory.</li>\n  <li><strong>Uniform Economic System:</strong> Internal customs duties and dues were abolished, and a uniform system of weights and measures was adopted.</li>\n  <li><strong>Promotion of Common National Language:</strong> Regional dialects were discouraged and French, as spoken in Paris, became the common language of the nation.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Concept of La Patrie & Le Citoyen with constitutional equality</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Adoption of Tricolour flag & renaming of National Assembly</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">New hymns, oaths, and martyr commemoration</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Abolition of internal duties & uniform weights/measures</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Standardisation of French as national language</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s1q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s1q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Who were Marianne and Germania? What was the importance of the way in which they were portrayed?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Identity of Marianne and Germania:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Marianne (France):</strong> Marianne was the female allegory of the French nation. Her characteristics were drawn from Liberty and the Republic — the red cap, the tricolour, and the cockade. Statues of Marianne were erected in public squares, coins, and postage stamps.</li>\n  <li><strong>Germania (Germany):</strong> Germania became the allegory of the German nation. In visual representations, Germania wears a crown of oak leaves, as the German oak stands for heroism.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Importance of their Portrayal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Female allegories gave the abstract idea of the nation a concrete physical form.</li>\n  <li>Portraying the nation as a motherland or heroic figure instilled feelings of collective belonging, devotion, and patriotic pride.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Marianne attributes (red cap, cockade, tricolour, coins/stamps)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Germania attributes (crown of oak leaves standing for heroism)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Analytical significance: Concrete physical embodiment of nationhood</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s1q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s1q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Briefly trace the process of German unification.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The process of German unification was completed through the following historic stages:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Failure of Liberal Middle-Class Effort (1848):</strong> The 1848 initiative by middle-class Germans to create a nation-state governed by an elected parliament at Frankfurt was crushed by the monarchy, military, and Prussian <em>Junkers</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Prussia Takes the Leadership:</strong> Prussia took on the leadership of national unification under Chief Minister <strong>Otto von Bismarck</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Policy of 'Blood and Iron':</strong> Bismarck followed a policy of militarism and diplomatic alliances to eliminate rival powers.</li>\n  <li><strong>Three Wars over Seven Years:</strong> Prussia fought three decisive wars over seven years — with Denmark (1864), Austria (1866), and France (1870–71) — all ending in Prussian victory.</li>\n  <li><strong>Proclamation of the German Empire (1871):</strong> On <strong>18 January 1871</strong>, in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles, Kaiser William I of Prussia was proclaimed Emperor of the united German Empire.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">1848 Frankfurt assembly failure & role of Junkers</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Prussian leadership & Otto von Bismarck as chief architect</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Militaristic policy of 'Blood and Iron'</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Three wars in seven years (Denmark, Austria, France)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">18 January 1871 proclamation of Kaiser William I at Versailles</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s1q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s1q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What changes did Napoleon introduce to make the administrative system more efficient in the territories ruled by him?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Napoleon introduced revolutionary administrative reforms known as the <strong>Civil Code of 1804 (Napoleonic Code)</strong> across France and conquered territories:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Abolition of Feudal Privileges:</strong> Abolished all privileges based on birth, established equality before the law, and secured property rights.</li>\n  <li><strong>Abolition of Serfdom and Manorial Dues:</strong> Simplified administrative divisions, abolished the feudal system, and freed peasants from serfdom and manorial dues.</li>\n  <li><strong>Removal of Guild Restrictions:</strong> In towns, guild restrictions were removed, promoting artisanal and mercantile freedom.</li>\n  <li><strong>Transport and Communication Improvements:</strong> Built modern roads, postal systems, and improved transport infrastructure.</li>\n  <li><strong>Uniform Laws and Currency:</strong> Standardised weights, measures, and a single national currency facilitated commerce and movement of goods.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Napoleonic Code of 1804: Equality before law & property rights</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Abolition of birth privileges & feudal system</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Liberation of peasants from serfdom and manorial dues</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Abolition of town guild restrictions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Uniform weights, measures, transport, and single national currency</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s1q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s1q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain what is meant by the 1848 revolution of the liberals. What were the political, social and economic ideas supported by the liberals?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Meaning of the 1848 Revolution of the Liberals:</strong></p>\n<p>In 1848, parallel to the revolts of the poor, unemployed, and starving peasants, a revolution led by the educated middle classes was under way in France, Germany, Italy, Poland, and Austria-Hungary demanding constitutionalism and national unification.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Ideas Supported by the Liberals:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Political Ideas:</strong> Government by consent, end of autocratic monarchy, written constitution, parliamentary representative government, and freedom of the press and association.</li>\n  <li><strong>Economic Ideas:</strong> Freedom of markets, abolition of state-imposed trade tariffs (as achieved by the <em>Zollverein</em> customs union), and protection of private property.</li>\n  <li><strong>Social Ideas:</strong> Equality before the law, though voting rights were initially restricted to property-owning men.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition and historical context of the 1848 liberal revolutions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Political tenets: Constitutionalism, parliamentary government, press freedom</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Economic tenets: Free markets, abolition of internal trade barriers, Zollverein</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Social tenets: Equality before law vs limitations on women's franchise</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s1q7\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s1q7')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q7</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How was the history of nationalism in Britain unlike the rest of Europe?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The formation of the nation-state in Britain was not the result of a sudden upheaval or revolution, but of a long-drawn-out parliamentary process:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Absence of a British Nation Prior to the 18th Century:</strong> Primary identities were ethnic (English, Welsh, Scot, Irish) with separate cultures.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ascendance of English Wealth and Power:</strong> The English nation steadily grew in wealth and power, extending its dominance.</li>\n  <li><strong>Parliamentary Supremacy (1688):</strong> The English parliament seized power from the monarchy in 1688, forging a nation-state with England at the centre.</li>\n  <li><strong>Act of Union (1707):</strong> The Act of Union between England and Scotland formed the 'United Kingdom of Great Britain', suppressing Scottish Gaelic culture and Highland traditions.</li>\n  <li><strong>Subjugation of Ireland (1801):</strong> Ireland was divided between Catholics and Protestants. Following Wolfe Tone’s revolt (1798), Ireland was forcibly incorporated into the UK in 1801.</li>\n  <li><strong>British Cultural Dominance:</strong> The Union Jack, the English anthem (<em>God Save Our Noble King</em>), and the English language were promoted.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ethnic identities vs absence of pre-18th century British nationhood</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">1688 Glorious Revolution and parliamentary supremacy</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Act of Union 1707 and suppression of Scottish Highlands</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Forced incorporation of Ireland in 1801 following Wolfe Tone's revolt</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Propagation of dominant English cultural symbols (Union Jack, Anthem)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s1q8\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s1q8')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q8</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why did nationalist tensions emerge in the Balkans?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The Balkans became the most serious source of nationalist tension in Europe after 1871 due to:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Geographical and Ethnic Diversity:</strong> Inhabited by diverse Slav populations (Romania, Bulgaria, Serbia, Greece, Croatia, etc.).</li>\n  <li><strong>Disintegration of the Ottoman Empire:</strong> The collapse of Ottoman control and spread of romantic nationalism made the area explosive.</li>\n  <li><strong>Failure of Modernisation:</strong> Subject nationalities broke away one by one and declared independence.</li>\n  <li><strong>Intense Mutual Jealousies:</strong> Balkan states were fiercely jealous and sought territorial expansion at each other’s expense.</li>\n  <li><strong>Big Power Rivalry:</strong> European powers (Russia, Germany, Britain, Austro-Hungary) competed over trade, colonies, and naval supremacy in the Balkans, culminating in the <strong>First World War (1914)</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ethnic composition of the Balkans (Slavic nationalities)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Disintegration and weakness of the Ottoman Empire</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Rise of Romantic Nationalism and assertions of historical independence</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Intense territorial jealousy among emergent Balkan states</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Imperial rivalry of Big Powers (Russia, Germany, Britain, Austria) leading to WWI</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 1</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Case-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'When conservative regimes were restored in Europe in 1815, they were autocratic. They did not tolerate criticism and dissent, and sought to curb activities that questioned the legitimacy of autocratic governments. Most of them imposed censorship laws to control what was said in newspapers, books, plays, and songs.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> How did liberal-nationalists respond to the suppression of free expression by conservative regimes after 1815?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Formation of Underground Secret Societies:</strong> Liberal-nationalists went underground, setting up secret societies (e.g., Mazzini's <em>Young Italy</em> and <em>Young Europe</em>) to train revolutionaries and spread ideas.</li><li><strong>Opposition to Monarchical Absolutism:</strong> Revolutionaries committed themselves to oppose autocratic monarchical forms restored by the Congress of Vienna and fight for liberty.</li><li><strong>Demand for Freedom of the Press:</strong> Freedom of the press remained a primary cause championed by liberal-nationalists against autocratic censorship.</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> Following the defeat of Napoleon in 1815, European governments were driven by a spirit of conservatism.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> Conservatives believed that established traditional institutions of state and society — like the monarchy, the Church, social hierarchies, property, and the family — should be preserved.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> Following the Congress of Vienna in 1815 hosted by Duke Metternich, European powers sought to undo revolutionary changes initiated by Napoleon and restore traditional autocratic monarchies to ensure European stability.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <div></div>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(2)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "2": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch2\" data-book=\"history\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">2</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">History: India and the Contemporary World – II</div>\n      <h2>Nationalism in India</h2>\n      <p>Unit 2: History • Non-Cooperation, Civil Disobedience, Satyagraha & Collective Belonging — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s2q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s2q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain: (a) Why growth of nationalism in the colonies is linked to an anti-colonial movement, (b) How the First World War helped in the growth of the National Movement in India, (c) Why Indians were outraged by the Rowlatt Act, (d) Why Gandhiji decided to withdraw the Non-Cooperation Movement.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>(a) Why growth of nationalism in the colonies is linked to an anti-colonial movement:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Shared Oppression:</strong> Colonised people began discovering their unity in the process of their struggle with colonialism. The sense of being oppressed under colonial rule provided a shared bond that tied many different groups together.</li>\n  <li><strong>Conflicting Experiences Unified:</strong> Although each class and group felt the effects of colonialism differently and their notions of freedom were not always the same, the common enemy (foreign British rule) united them under a common national platform forged by the Congress and Mahatma Gandhi.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(b) How the First World War helped in the growth of the National Movement in India:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Huge Increase in Defence Expenditure:</strong> The war created a new economic and political situation. It led to a huge increase in defence expenditure, which was financed by war loans and increasing taxes — customs duties were raised and income tax was introduced for the first time.</li>\n  <li><strong>Price Rise and Extreme Hardship:</strong> Between 1913 and 1918, prices doubled, leading to extreme hardship for the common people.</li>\n  <li><strong>Forced Recruitment in Rural Areas:</strong> Villages were called upon to supply soldiers, and the forced recruitment in rural areas caused widespread anger and resentment.</li>\n  <li><strong>Crop Failure and Influenza Epidemic:</strong> In 1918–19 and 1920–21, crops failed in many parts of India, resulting in acute shortages of food. This was accompanied by an influenza epidemic. According to the census of 1921, 12 to 13 million people perished as a result of famines and the epidemic. When hardships did not end after the war as promised, people rallied behind the national movement.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(c) Why Indians were outraged by the Rowlatt Act (1919):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Hurried Enactment despite Opposition:</strong> The Rowlatt Act was hurriedly passed through the Imperial Legislative Council despite the united opposition of the Indian members.</li>\n  <li><strong>Enormous Repressive Powers:</strong> It gave the British government enormous powers to repress political activities, and allowed detention of political prisoners without trial for up to two years.</li>\n  <li><strong>Violation of Fundamental Liberty:</strong> It denied the fundamental legal safeguard of <em>habeas corpus</em> and was condemned as a 'Black Act' aimed at crushing civil liberties.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(d) Why Gandhiji decided to withdraw the Non-Cooperation Movement (1922):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Turn towards Violence (Chauri Chaura Incident):</strong> In February 1922, Mahatma Gandhi decided to withdraw the Non-Cooperation Movement because he felt the movement was turning violent in many places. At Chauri Chaura in Gorakhpur (Uttar Pradesh), a peaceful demonstration in a bazaar turned into a violent clash with the police, resulting in the burning of a police station and the death of 22 policemen.</li>\n  <li><strong>Need for Mass Training in Satyagraha:</strong> Gandhiji realized that <em>satyagrahis</em> needed to be properly trained before they would be ready for genuine mass struggles, and violence gave the colonial state an excuse for ruthless armed repression.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Link between anti-colonialism and nationalism in colonies</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">First World War economic impacts (taxes, inflation, forced recruitment)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Rowlatt Act repressive clauses (2-year detention without trial)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Chauri Chaura incident & rationale for withdrawing Non-Cooperation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s2q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s2q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is meant by the idea of Satyagraha?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The idea of <strong>Satyagraha</strong> formulated by Mahatma Gandhi is based on the following fundamental core principles:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Power of Truth:</strong> The idea of Satyagraha emphasized the power of truth and the need to search for truth. It suggested that if the cause was true, if the struggle was against injustice, then physical force was not necessary to fight the oppressor.</li>\n  <li><strong>Pure Non-Violence (Ahimsa):</strong> Without being aggressive, without revenge or malice, a satyagrahi could win the battle through non-violence. This could be done by appealing directly to the conscience of the oppressor.</li>\n  <li><strong>Persuading the Oppressor:</strong> People — including the oppressors — had to be persuaded to see the truth, instead of being forced to accept it through the use of violence. By this struggle, truth was bound to ultimately triumph.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dharma of Universal Unity:</strong> Mahatma Gandhi firmly believed that this <em>dharma</em> of non-violence could unite all Indians regardless of caste, creed, or religion.</li>\n  <li><strong>Successful Early Applications:</strong> Gandhiji successfully organised Satyagraha movements in Champaran (Bihar, 1917) against oppressive indigo plantation system, Kheda (Gujarat, 1917) for revenue remission following crop failure, and Ahmedabad (1918) for cotton mill workers' wage demands.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Emphasis on power of truth and searching for truth</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Absence of malice, hatred or physical force (Ahimsa)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Appealing to the conscience of the oppressor</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Belief in ultimate triumph of truth & universal dharma</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Early experimental victories in Champaran, Kheda, and Ahmedabad</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s2q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s2q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Write a newspaper report on: (a) The Jallianwala Bagh massacre (b) The Simon Commission.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Newspaper Report: The Jallianwala Bagh Massacre (Amritsar, 13 April 1919)</strong></p>\n<p><em>Amritsar, 14 April 1919:</em> Yesterday, on the holy festive day of Baisakhi, one of the most horrific atrocities in colonial history was perpetrated at Jallianwala Bagh in Amritsar. A large, peaceful crowd had gathered in the enclosed ground — some to protest against the government's repressive Rowlatt Act and the arrest of leaders Dr. Saifuddin Kitchlew and Dr. Satyapal, while others had arrived from surrounding villages to attend the Baisakhi fair, unaware of the martial law imposed by General Dyer. Dyer entered the area with armed troops, blocked all narrow exit points, and without giving any warning, ordered his men to open fire on the trapped, unarmed crowd. Hundreds of innocent men, women, and children were butchered on the spot, and over a thousand were severely injured. General Dyer later declared brazenly that his objective was to 'produce a moral effect' and strike terror into the hearts of satyagrahis. Widespread strikes, clashes with police, and government bombings followed across Punjab, leading Rabindranath Tagore to renounce his British Knighthood in sheer protest.</p>\n\n<p><strong>2. Newspaper Report: Protests against the Simon Commission (Bombay/Lahore, 1928)</strong></p>\n<p><em>Lahore, 31 October 1928:</em> Massive nationwide protests erupted across Indian cities as the Indian Statutory Commission, headed by Sir John Simon, arrived in India. Set up by the British Conservative government to review the working of the constitutional system and suggest changes, the Commission has provoked absolute fury across all political spectrums — including the Indian National Congress and the Muslim League. The central reason for public outrage is that the Commission does not contain a single Indian member; all seven members are British politicians. Wherever the Commission arrived, it was greeted with black-flag demonstrations, mass hartals, and loud chants of <strong>'Simon Go Back'</strong>. During a peaceful demonstration in Lahore led by veteran nationalist leader <strong>Lala Lajpat Rai</strong>, police under Superintendent Scott unleashed a brutal lathi-charge. Lala Lajpat Rai sustained grave chest injuries and subsequently succumbed, declaring that <em>'every blow aimed at me is a nail in the coffin of British imperialism'</em>.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Jallianwala Bagh context, General Dyer's actions & martial law</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Impact of massacre: Casualties, moral terror, Tagore's Knighthood renunciation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Simon Commission composition flaw (All-white, no Indian member)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Nationwide protests, 'Simon Go Back' slogan, and Lala Lajpat Rai's martyrdom</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s2q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s2q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Compare the images of Bharat Mata in this chapter with the image of Germania in Chapter 1.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison between Bharat Mata and Germania:</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;margin-top:10px;\">\n  <tr style=\"background:#f0fdfa;color:#115e59;\">\n    <th>Feature</th>\n    <th>Bharat Mata (India)</th>\n    <th>Germania (Germany)</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Creation &amp; Painter</strong></td>\n    <td>First created by Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay in <em>Vande Mataram</em>; famously painted by <strong>Abanindranath Tagore</strong> (1905) and later popularised by others.</td>\n    <td>Visualised by Philipp Veit (1848) on a cotton banner hanging from the ceiling of St. Paul's Church in Frankfurt.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Attributes &amp; Symbolism</strong></td>\n    <td>In Tagore’s painting, she is portrayed as an ascetic figure: calm, composed, divine, and spiritual. She dispenses learning, food, and clothing. In later popular prints, she holds a trishul, stands beside a lion and an elephant — symbols of power and authority.</td>\n    <td>Germania wears a crown of oak leaves (standing for heroism), carries a sword (readiness to fight), an olive branch around the sword (willingness to make peace), black-red-gold tricolour, and breastplate with an eagle (symbol of German imperial strength).</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>National Purpose</strong></td>\n    <td>Devotion to Bharat Mata became an evidence of one's nationalism; it unified diverse linguistic and religious communities against British colonial subjugation.</td>\n    <td>Visual embodiment of a unified, sovereign German Reich, inspiring martial heroism and resistance against foreign domination.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Origin & creators of both allegories (Tagore/Bankim vs Veit)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Visual attributes comparison (Ascetic/Trishul/Lion vs Oak leaves/Sword/Eagle)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Nationalistic impact on citizens' consciousness</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s2q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s2q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">List all the different social groups that joined the Non-Cooperation Movement of 1921. Then choose any three and write about their hopes and struggles to show why they joined the movement.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Social Groups Participating in the Non-Cooperation Movement (1921):</strong></p>\n<p>Middle-class citizens in towns (students, teachers, lawyers), peasants in Awadh, tribal peasants in the Gudem Hills of Andhra Pradesh, and plantation workers in Assam.</p>\n\n<p><strong>2. Detailed Analysis of Three Chosen Social Groups:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>(a) Peasants of Awadh (Led by Baba Ramchandra):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Hopes and Grievances:</em> The movement was against <em>talukdars</em> and landlords who demanded exorbitantly high rents and a variety of other cesses. Peasants had to do <em>begar</em> (unpaid forced labour) and work at landlords’ farms without any payment. As tenants, they had no security of tenure and were regularly evicted.</li>\n      <li><em>Struggle &amp; Action:</em> The peasant movement demanded reduction of revenue, abolition of <em>begar</em>, and social boycott of oppressive landlords. In many places, <em>nai-dhobi bandhs</em> were organised. In 1920, the <em>Oudh Kisan Sabha</em> was set up headed by Jawaharlal Nehru and Baba Ramchandra. However, as the movement spread in 1921, the houses of talukdars and merchants were attacked, bazaars were looted, and grain hoards were taken over, which alarmed the Congress leadership.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(b) Tribal Peasants of Gudem Hills, Andhra Pradesh (Led by Alluri Sitaram Raju):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Hopes and Grievances:</em> In the Gudem Hills, a militant guerrilla movement spread. The colonial government had closed large forest areas, preventing people from entering the forests to graze cattle or collect fuelwood and fruits. This enraged the hill people, affecting their livelihoods and traditional rights. When the government began forcing them to contribute <em>begar</em> for road building, they revolted.</li>\n      <li><em>Struggle &amp; Action:</em> Their leader, Alluri Sitaram Raju, claimed special powers (astrological predictions, healing, surviving bullet shots). Inspired by Gandhiji, he persuaded people to wear <em>khadi</em> and give up drinking. But he asserted that India could be liberated only by the use of force, not non-violence. The Gudem rebels attacked police stations, attempted to kill British officials, and carried on guerrilla warfare until Raju was captured and executed in 1924.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(c) Plantation Workers in Assam:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Hopes and Grievances:</em> Under the <strong>Inland Emigration Act of 1859</strong>, plantation workers were not permitted to leave tea gardens without permission, and in fact they were rarely given such permission. For workers, freedom meant the right to move freely in and out of the confined spaces in which they were enclosed, and retaining a link with their native villages.</li>\n      <li><em>Struggle &amp; Action:</em> When they heard of the Non-Cooperation Movement, thousands of workers defied the authorities, left the plantations, and headed home. They believed that 'Gandhi Raj' was coming and everyone would be given land in their own villages. Tragically, stranded on the way due to railway and steamer strikes, they were caught by the police and brutally beaten up.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identification of diverse participating social groups</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Awadh peasants: Talukdars, begar, Oudh Kisan Sabha & deviation into violence</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Gudem hill tribals: Forest restrictions, Raju's leadership & guerrilla war</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Assam plantation workers: Inland Emigration Act 1859 & conception of Swaraj</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s2q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s2q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Discuss the Salt March to make clear why it was an effective symbol of resistance against colonialism.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Mahatma Gandhi found in <strong>salt</strong> a remarkably powerful and effective symbol that could unite the entire nation against colonial exploitation:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Universal Consumption Across All Classes:</strong> Salt was an essential item of food consumed equally by the rich and the poor alike. The tax on salt and the government monopoly over its production revealed the most oppressive face of British rule.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ultimatum to Viceroy Irwin:</strong> On 31 January 1930, Gandhiji sent a letter to Viceroy Irwin stating eleven demands, the most stirring of which was the demand to abolish the salt tax. When Irwin was unwilling to negotiate, Gandhiji launched the historic March.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Dandi March (12 March – 6 April 1930):</strong> Accompanied by 78 of his trusted volunteers, Gandhiji walked 240 miles from his ashram in Sabarmati to the Gujarati coastal town of Dandi over 24 days (about 10 miles a day). Thousands came to hear Gandhiji wherever he stopped.</li>\n  <li><strong>Defying Colonial Law:</strong> On 6 April 1930, Gandhiji reached Dandi, ritually manufactured salt by boiling sea water, and formally broke the colonial salt law. This marked the dramatic beginning of the <strong>Civil Disobedience Movement</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Mobilisation of Entire Society:</strong> Breaking the salt law inspired millions across India to manufacture salt in front of government salt factories, boycott foreign cloth, picket liquor shops, refuse to pay <em>chaukidari</em> taxes, and violate forest laws. It transformed the national struggle into an active mass defiance of unjust imperial laws.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Universal indispensability of salt bridging rich-poor divide</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Letter of 11 demands to Viceroy Irwin & abolition of salt tax</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Dandi March details (78 volunteers, 240 miles, 24 days)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ritual boiling of sea water on 6 April 1930</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Catalytic impact: Sparking nationwide Civil Disobedience Movement</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s2q7\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s2q7')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q7</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Imagine you are a woman participating in the Civil Disobedience Movement. Explain what the experience meant to your life.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Perspective of a Woman Participant in the Civil Disobedience Movement:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Breaking Out of Domestic Seclusion:</strong> Participating in the Civil Disobedience Movement was a transformative milestone in my life. Before Gandhiji's call, society expected women to remain confined within the four walls of the home, limited to child-rearing and domestic chores.</li>\n  <li><strong>Answering Gandhiji's Call:</strong> When Gandhiji marched to Dandi and called upon women to serve the nation, thousands of us came out of our homes to listen to him. We saw service to the nation as a sacred religious duty (<em>dharma</em>) of women.</li>\n  <li><strong>Active Ground-Level Participation:</strong> In urban areas, we women from high-caste families, and in rural areas, women from rich peasant households, participated in protest marches, manufactured salt, and courageously picketed foreign cloth and liquor shops. Many of us were baton-charged by the police and willingly went to jail.</li>\n  <li><strong>New Sense of Self-Worth and Dignity:</strong> This experience instilled immense self-confidence and political awareness in us. Although the Congress leadership was initially reluctant to grant women positions of authority within the organisation and viewed our role primarily as symbolic, our active participation permanently changed the public perception of women's strength and role in India's freedom struggle.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Transition from traditional domestic sphere to national politics</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Active protest methods: Salt manufacturing, picketing liquor/cloth shops</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Facing police lathis and imprisonment</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Long-term impact: Enhanced self-worth & shifting societal perceptions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s2q8\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s2q8')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q8</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why did political leaders differ sharply over the question of separate electorates?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Political leaders differed sharply over the question of separate electorates because of conflicting visions of community representation and national unity:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Dr. B.R. Ambedkar and Dalit Representation:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Dr. B.R. Ambedkar, who organised the Dalits into the Depressed Classes Association in 1930, clashed with Mahatma Gandhi at the second Round Table Conference by demanding separate electorates for Dalits.</li>\n      <li>Ambedkar argued that political empowerment was the only genuine path to resolve the social backwardness, untouchability, and historical oppression of Dalits.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Mahatma Gandhi’s Opposition and Fast unto Death:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Gandhiji vehemently opposed separate electorates for Dalits, arguing that it would permanently segregate the Dalits from mainstream Hindu society and fatally disrupt national integration.</li>\n      <li>When the British government conceded Ambedkar’s demand under the Communal Award, Gandhiji began a fast unto death in Yerwada Jail (Pune).</li>\n      <li>This resulted in the historic <strong>Poona Pact (September 1932)</strong>, where Ambedkar accepted joint electorates in exchange for reserved seats for the Depressed Classes in provincial and central legislative councils.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Muhammad Ali Jinnah and Muslim League Demands:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Muhammad Ali Jinnah and the Muslim League insisted on separate electorates or reserved seats in proportion to population in Muslim-majority provinces (Bengal and Punjab) and one-third representation in the Central Assembly.</li>\n      <li>They feared that under a joint electorate with a Hindu majority, the culture, identity, and political voice of the Muslim minority would be submerged.</li>\n      <li>Negotiations broke down completely in 1928 when M.R. Jayakar of the Hindu Mahasabha strongly opposed any compromise on the issue.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ambedkar's stance: Social emancipation through separate electorates</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Gandhiji's opposition: Fear of permanent societal division & fast unto death</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Poona Pact 1932 compromise: Reserved seats within joint electorates</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Muslim League / Jinnah's perspective and breakdown of consensus</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 2</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Source-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'It is said of 'passive resistance' that it is the weapon of the weak, but the power which is the subject of this article can be used only by the strong. This power is not passive resistance; indeed it calls for intense activity. The movement in South Africa was not passive, but active... Satyagraha is not physical force. A satyagrah does not inflict pain on the adversary; he does not seek his destruction... In the use of satyagraha, there is no ill-will whatever.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> How does Mahatma Gandhi distinguish Satyagraha from passive resistance in this text?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Active Soul-Force, Not Passive Submission:</strong> Passive resistance is often considered a defensive weapon of the physically weak, whereas Satyagraha is the weapon of the morally strong, requiring immense courage, active resistance, and unyielding truth-force.</li><li><strong>Total Renunciation of Malice:</strong> Unlike passive resistance, which may harbor latent hatred or use violence if opportunity permits, Satyagraha is rooted purely in soul-force and love. It seeks to convert the oppressor through voluntary self-suffering without causing any injury or ill-will to the adversary.</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> The Simon Commission was greeted with the slogan 'Simon Go Back' when it arrived in India in 1928.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> The Commission did not have a single Indian member; all members were British.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> The British government appointed the statutory commission under Sir John Simon to review the Indian constitutional system, but deliberately excluded all Indians. Indian leaders felt deeply insulted that foreigners were deciding India's constitutional destiny, prompting unanimous nationwide protests and black-flag demonstrations.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(1)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(3)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "3": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch3\" data-book=\"history\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">3</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">History: India and the Contemporary World – II</div>\n      <h2>The Making of a Global World</h2>\n      <p>Unit 3: History • Pre-modern Silk Routes, Colonisation, Rinderpest & Global Economy — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s3q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s3q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Give two examples of different types of global exchanges which took place before the seventeenth century, choosing one example from Asia and one from the Americas.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Pre-seventeenth century global exchanges:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>From Asia (The Silk Routes):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>The Silk Routes are a prime example of vibrant pre-modern trade and cultural links connecting Asia with Europe and northern Africa.</li>\n      <li>Chinese silk cargoes, Indian spices, and Chinese pottery travelled along these maritime and overland routes to Western markets. In return, precious metals — gold and silver — flowed from Europe to Asia. Alongside trade, cultural and religious exchanges took place: Buddhism from India spread in several directions through intersecting points on the silk routes.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>From the Americas (Introduction of New Food Crops):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Before Christopher Columbus accidentally discovered the vast continent later known as the Americas, many of our common foods were unknown.</li>\n      <li>Potatoes, maize, tomatoes, chillies, soya, groundnuts, and sweet potatoes were introduced to Europe and Asia from the Americas. These new crops transformed European lives: the humble potato significantly improved the nutrition and life expectancy of Europe’s poorest peasants (e.g., Ireland's peasants became so dependent on potatoes that when disease destroyed the crop in the mid-1840s, hundreds of thousands died of starvation during the Great Irish Potato Famine).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Asian trade: Silk routes, textiles, spices, and precious metal return flows</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">American exchange: Introduction of new crops (potato, maize, chillies) and Irish famine</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s3q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s3q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain how the global transfer of disease in the pre-modern world helped in the colonisation of the Americas.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The global transfer of disease in the pre-modern world proved to be the deadliest weapon in the European colonisation of the Americas:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Biological Warfare via Germs:</strong> The Portuguese and Spanish conquest and colonisation of America was decisively under way by the mid-sixteenth century. European conquest was not just a result of superior military firepower; in fact, the most powerful weapon of the Spanish conquerors was not a conventional military weapon at all. It was the germs such as those of <strong>smallpox</strong> that they carried on their person.</li>\n  <li><strong>Lack of Immunity among Native Americans:</strong> Because of their long isolation from the rest of the world, America’s original inhabitants had no immunity against these diseases that came from Europe.</li>\n  <li><strong>Decimation of Entire Communities:</strong> Smallpox in particular proved a deadly killer. Once introduced into the continent, it spread deep into the interior, even ahead of any Europeans reaching there. It decimated and wiped out whole communities, paving the way for effortless conquest.</li>\n  <li><strong>Invulnerability of European Conquerors:</strong> Guns and firearms could be bought or captured and turned against the invaders. But diseases like smallpox could not be fought back because the European conquerors were mostly immune to it, whereas the indigenous defenders had no biological defense.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Role of biological agents over conventional military weapons</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Historical isolation of native Americans & complete lack of immunity</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Rapid transmission of smallpox killing indigenous populations</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">One-sided biological advantage enabling Spanish/Portuguese colonisation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s3q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s3q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Write an educational note to explain the effects of the following: (a) The British government's decision to abolish the Corn Laws, (b) The coming of rinderpest to Africa, (c) The death of men of working age in Europe because of the World War, (d) The Great Depression on the Indian economy, (e) The decision of MNCs to relocate production to Asian countries.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>(a) Abolition of the Corn Laws in Britain:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Under pressure from urban industrialists and city dwellers, the British government abolished the Corn Laws (tariffs on imported food).</li>\n  <li><em>Effects:</em> Food could now be imported into Britain much more cheaply than it could be produced within the country. British agriculture was unable to compete with imports. Vast areas of land were left uncultivated, and thousands of farm labourers lost their jobs, migrating to cities or emigrating overseas. However, lower food prices led to higher consumption and stimulated global agricultural trade.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(b) The Coming of Rinderpest (Cattle Plague) to Africa:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Rinderpest arrived in Africa in the late 1880s, carried by infected cattle imported from British Asia to feed Italian soldiers invading Eritrea.</li>\n  <li><em>Effects:</em> Moving like forest fire, it killed <strong>90 per cent</strong> of Africa's cattle. The loss of cattle destroyed African livelihoods, devastated their subsistence economy, and forced previously self-reliant Africans into the labour market, allowing European colonisers to easily conquer and monopolise the continent.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(c) Death of Working-Age Men in Europe during the First World War:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The First World War was the first modern industrial war, utilizing machine guns, tanks, aircraft, and chemical weapons. Most of the 9 million dead and 20 million injured were men of working age.</li>\n  <li><em>Effects:</em> These deaths drastically reduced the able-bodied workforce in Europe. Household incomes plummeted. Furthermore, as men went to battle, women stepped in to undertake jobs that earlier only men were expected to do, leading to a profound transformation in women's social role.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(d) The Great Depression on the Indian Economy (1929–1934):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The Depression immediately affected Indian trade. India’s exports and imports nearly halved between 1928 and 1934.</li>\n  <li><em>Effects:</em> Wheat prices in India fell by 50 per cent. Peasants and farmers suffered more than urban dwellers; though agricultural prices collapsed, colonial authorities refused to reduce land revenue demands. Peasants used up their savings, mortgaged lands, and sold gold and jewellery (turning India into an exporter of precious metal), leading to widespread rural indebtedness.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(e) Relocation of Production by MNCs to Asian Countries:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>From the late 1970s, multinational corporations began relocating production operations to low-wage Asian countries like China, India, and Southeast Asia.</li>\n  <li><em>Effects:</em> Wages were relatively low in countries like China. This stimulated world trade and capital flows, created enormous employment opportunities in Asian economies, and resulted in rapid economic transformation, making countries like China and India major hubs of global manufacturing and services.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Corn laws abolition: Cheap grain imports, farm displacement, and urbanization</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Rinderpest: 90% cattle death and subjugation of African labour</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">WWI working-age deaths: Labour shortage and rise of female employment</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Great Depression on India: Export collapse, price drop, rural distress</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">MNC relocation: Low-wage advantage and rise of Asian manufacturing hubs</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s3q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s3q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Give two examples from history to show the impact of technology on food availability.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Technology played a revolutionary role in improving food availability across nineteenth-century global markets, as shown by these two major historical examples:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Development of Refrigerated Ships:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Earlier Condition:</em> Till the 1870s, animals were shipped live from America to Europe and then slaughtered. Live animals took up immense ship space, many died in voyage, fell ill, or became too lean to eat. Meat was consequently an expensive luxury beyond the reach of European poor.</li>\n      <li><em>Technological Breakthrough:</em> The invention of refrigerated ships enabled the transport of perishable foods over long distances. Animals could now be slaughtered at the starting point (in America, Australia, or New Zealand) and then transported to Europe as frozen meat. This reduced shipping costs, lowered meat prices in European markets, and allowed poor workers to consume a varied diet including meat, butter, and eggs.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Railways and Faster Steamships:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Railways linked distant agricultural hinterlands to coastal ports, while larger, faster steamships transported bulk food grains across oceans quickly and cheaply.</li>\n      <li>Raw food items like wheat from the American prairies, Russian steppes, and Australian plains could be transported in massive quantities to feed urban industrial populations in Western Europe without spoiling.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Refrigerated ships: Shifting from live animal transport to frozen meat</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Impact: Drastic drop in meat prices, improved nutrition for European poor</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Railways & fast steamships connecting interior grain belts to global ports</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s3q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s3q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is meant by the Bretton Woods Agreement? Explain its significance.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Meaning of the Bretton Woods Agreement:</strong></p>\n<p>The Bretton Woods Agreement was finalized in July 1944 at the United Nations Monetary and Financial Conference held at Bretton Woods in New Hampshire, USA. Its main aim was to preserve economic stability and full employment in the industrial world after the devastation of the Second World War.</p>\n\n<p><strong>2. Core Bretton Woods Institutions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>International Monetary Fund (IMF):</strong> Established to deal with external surpluses and deficits of its member nations.</li>\n  <li><strong>International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (World Bank):</strong> Set up to finance post-war reconstruction of war-shattered economies.</li>\n  <li>These two institutions are commonly known as the <strong>Bretton Woods Twins</strong>. They commenced financial operations in 1947. Decision-making was dominated by Western industrial powers, particularly the US, which held effective veto power over key decisions.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>3. Historical Significance:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Inaugurated an era of unprecedented growth of trade and incomes for Western industrial nations and Japan between 1950 and the early 1970s.</li>\n  <li>Fixed exchange rates based on the US dollar anchored to gold at a fixed price ($35 per ounce of gold).</li>\n  <li>When developing nations later realized that IMF and World Bank were controlled by Western powers, they organised themselves as the <strong>Group of 77 (G-77)</strong> to demand a <strong>New International Economic Order (NIEO)</strong> with real control over natural resources and fairer trade terms.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Context and objectives of July 1944 Bretton Woods Conference</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">The Bretton Woods Twins: Functions of IMF & World Bank</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Significance: Fixed exchange rates & formation of G-77 by developing nations</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 3</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Case-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'The Great Depression began around 1929 and lasted till the mid-1930s. During this period, most parts of the world experienced catastrophic declines in production, employment, incomes and trade. Agricultural regions and communities were the most severely affected. This was because the fall in agricultural prices was greater and more prolonged than that in the prices of industrial goods.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> Why were agricultural regions hit harder than industrial sectors during the Great Depression?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Agricultural Overproduction and Price Collapse:</strong> Agricultural overproduction remained a severe problem. As prices slumped, farmers tried to expand production and bring larger volumes of produce to the market to maintain overall income. This flooded the market further, pushing down prices even more drastically.</li><li><strong>Inability to Cut Output Instantly:</strong> Unlike industrial factories that could shut down machines, lay off workers, and curtail manufacturing instantly, agricultural production depends on biological cycles and cannot be halted abruptly, leaving farmers trapped with massive unsellable stocks.</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> Nineteenth-century 'indentured labour migration' has often been described as a 'new system of slavery'.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> Agents gave false information to poor migrants about final destinations, modes of travel, the nature of the work, and living conditions; on arrival, labourers found harsh living conditions and few legal rights.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> Hundreds of thousands of Indian and Chinese indentured labourers were recruited under contracts promising return travel after 5 years of work in Caribbean plantations, Fiji, and Mauritius. In reality, recruiters tricked illiterate peasants, wages were extremely low, deductions were heavy, and any attempt to escape was punished with severe imprisonment and corporal punishment, mirroring chattel slavery.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(2)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(4)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "4": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch4\" data-book=\"history\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">4</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">History: India and the Contemporary World – II</div>\n      <h2>The Age of Industrialisation</h2>\n      <p>Unit 4: History • Proto-industrialisation, Factories, Hand Labour & Indian Mills — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s4q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s4q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the following: (a) Women workers in Britain attacked the Spinning Jenny, (b) In the seventeenth century merchants from towns in Europe began employing peasants and artisans within the villages, (c) The port of Surat declined by the end of the eighteenth century, (d) The East India Company appointed gomasthas to supervise weavers in India.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>(a) Women workers in Britain attacked the Spinning Jenny:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Invented by <strong>James Hargreaves</strong> in 1764, the Spinning Jenny speeded up the spinning process and reduced labour demand. A single wheel could turn a number of spindles and spin several threads simultaneously.</li>\n  <li>Hand-spinning was the primary livelihood of thousands of women workers in Britain. The fear of unemployment made women workers hostile to the new machine. When the Spinning Jenny was introduced in the woollen industry, women who survived on hand spinning began attacking the machines.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(b) In the seventeenth century, merchants from towns in Europe began employing peasants and artisans within the villages:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>In towns, urban craft and trade guilds were extremely powerful. These associations of producers trained craftspersons, maintained control over production, regulated competition and prices, and restricted the entry of new people into the trade.</li>\n  <li>Monarchs granted different guilds the monopoly right to produce and trade in specific products. It was therefore difficult for new merchants to set up business in towns. So they turned to the countryside, supplying money to poor peasants and artisans and persuading them to produce for an international market.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(c) The port of Surat declined by the end of the eighteenth century:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Before the age of machine industries, Surat on the Gujarat coast connected India to the Gulf and Red Sea ports through a vibrant network controlled by Indian merchants and bankers.</li>\n  <li>By the 1750s, this network was breaking down. European companies gradually gained power — first securing a variety of concessions from local courts, and then the monopoly rights to trade. They operated through their own chosen ports like Bombay and Calcutta. As credit dried up and local bankers went bankrupt, trade through Surat slumped dramatically (gross value of trade fell from Rs 16 million in the late 17th century to Rs 3 million by the 1740s).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(d) The East India Company appointed Gomasthas to supervise weavers in India:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The East India Company wanted to eliminate competition from existing traders, French, Dutch, and Portuguese, and secure a direct, unbroken monopoly over the supply of cotton and silk textiles.</li>\n  <li>It established a system of direct management and control by appointing a paid servant called the <strong>Gomastha</strong> to supervise weavers, collect supplies, and examine the quality of cloth, preventing weavers from dealing with other buyers through coercive advance loan systems.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Spinning Jenny mechanism & women's unemployment fears</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Urban guild monopolies driving merchants into countryside</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Rise of European trading companies & shift to Bombay/Calcutta</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Gomasthas role in eliminating competition & enforcing loans</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s4q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s4q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">State whether True or False with detailed historical reasoning: (a) 80% industrial workforce in 19th-century Europe, (b) Indian dominance of fine textiles, (c) American Civil War and cotton exports, (d) The fly shuttle and handloom productivity.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Examination of Historical Statements (True / False with Explanations):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a) At the end of the nineteenth century, 80 per cent of the total workforce in Europe was employed in the technologically advanced industrial sector.</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>FALSE:</strong> Even by the late nineteenth century, less than 20 per cent of the total workforce was employed in technologically advanced industrial sectors. Traditional industries like food processing, pottery, glasswork, and furniture making employed the vast bulk of labourers.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(b) The international market for fine textiles was dominated by India till the eighteenth century.</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>TRUE:</strong> Indian cotton and silk goods dominated the international market in quality and craftsmanship before mechanical industrialisation in Britain. Armenian and Persian merchants took goods from Punjab to Afghanistan, eastern Persia, and Central Asia.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(c) The American Civil War resulted in the reduction of cotton exports from India.</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>FALSE:</strong> When the American Civil War broke out in 1861, raw cotton supplies from the US to Britain were cut off. Britain turned urgently to India. As raw cotton exports from India soared, prices skyrocketed, causing extreme hardship to Indian handloom weavers who could not afford expensive raw cotton.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(d) The introduction of the fly shuttle increased the productivity of handloom workers without pushing up labour demand.</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>TRUE:</strong> The fly shuttle was a mechanical device operated by ropes and pullies that speeded up weaving. It enabled handloom weavers to operate large looms and weave wide pieces of cloth rapidly, increasing output per worker without increasing overall labour costs.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Statement (a) validation with proportion of industrial workers</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Statement (b) validation with Indian textile dominance</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Statement (c) correction on American Civil War boom</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Statement (d) explanation on fly shuttle productivity</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s4q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s4q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain what is meant by proto-industrialisation.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Meaning of Proto-Industrialisation:</strong></p>\n<p>Proto-industrialisation refers to the phase of industrialisation that existed before the emergence of modern mechanized factories. There was a large-scale industrial production for an international market that was not based in factories, but centered in peasant households across the countryside.</p>\n\n<p><strong>2. Key Features and Working Mechanism:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Merchant-Producer Relationship:</strong> Merchants were based in towns, but the work was done mostly in the countryside. A merchant clothier in England bought wool from a wool stapler, carried it to the spinners, the yarn that was spun was taken to fullers, and then to dyers.</li>\n  <li><strong>London as the Finishing Centre:</strong> The finishing of the cloth was done in London before the export merchant sold the cloth in the international market. London in fact came to be known as a <em>finishing centre</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Supplementary Income for Farming Households:</strong> In the countryside, poor peasants and cottagers eagerly worked for merchants because open fields were disappearing and commons were being enclosed. Tiny family plots could not provide enough work for all members; working for proto-industrial merchants allowed them to remain in the countryside and cultivate their small plots while earning supplementary income.</li>\n  <li><strong>Family Labour:</strong> The whole family — men, women, and children — participated in different stages of the production process.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition: Pre-factory phase of mass production for world market</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Network of production: Stapler, spinner, weaver, fuller, dyer</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">London as the global finishing centre</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Socio-economic integration with rural peasant livelihoods</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s4q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s4q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why did some industrialists in nineteenth-century Europe prefer hand labour over machines?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>In nineteenth-century Europe (especially Britain), many industrialists preferred hand labour over machines due to the following compelling economic reasons:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Abundance of Cheap Labour:</strong> Poor peasants and vagrants moved to cities in large numbers in search of work. When labour was plentiful, wages were extremely low. Industrialists had no incentive to invest heavily in expensive machinery that required costly maintenance.</li>\n  <li><strong>Seasonal Demand for Labour:</strong> In many industries, demand for labour was seasonal. Gas works and breweries were especially busy through the cold winter months. Bookbinders and printers needed extra hands before Christmas. In all such seasonal industries, employers preferred hand labour, hiring workers only for the peak season rather than buying machines that sat idle for the rest of the year.</li>\n  <li><strong>Demand for Intricate and Varied Designs:</strong> A large range of products could be produced only with hand labour. Machines were oriented to producing uniform, standardized goods for a mass market. But the market often demanded goods with specific shapes, intricate designs, and colours (e.g., 500 varieties of hammers and 45 kinds of axes produced in Britain), which required human skill, not mechanical uniformity.</li>\n  <li><strong>Aristocratic Taste for Handmade Goods:</strong> In Victorian Britain, the upper classes — the aristocrats and the bourgeoisie — preferred things produced by hand. Handmade goods came to symbolize refinement, class, and exclusivity. They were individually made and carefully finished. Machine-made goods were considered cheap and meant for export to the colonies.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Abundance of low-wage human labour in urban centres</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Seasonal fluctuation in production (breweries, gas works, printing)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Need for intricate designs and specialized varieties</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Victorian aristocratic preference for handcrafted refinement</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s4q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s4q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did the East India Company procure regular supplies of cotton and silk textiles from Indian weavers?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The English East India Company established a coercive system of managerial and political control to procure regular supplies of cotton and silk textiles from Indian weavers:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Eliminating Competitors:</strong> The Company first established political power, which enabled it to assert a monopoly right to trade. It eliminated existing traders and brokers connected with the cloth trade.</li>\n  <li><strong>Appointment of Gomasthas:</strong> It appointed a paid servant called the <em>Gomastha</em> to supervise weavers, collect supplies, and examine the quality of cloth.</li>\n  <li><strong>System of Advances (Tied Credit):</strong> It prevented Company weavers from dealing with other buyers through the system of advances. Once an order was placed, weavers were given loans to purchase raw materials for their production. Those who took loans had to hand over the cloth they produced exclusively to the Gomastha. They could not sell it to any other trader.</li>\n  <li><strong>Loss of Independence and Land:</strong> Many weavers had small plots of land that they earlier cultivated alongside weaving. The demand for Company cloth forced them to lease out their lands and devote all their time to weaving. Entire families — children and women — were constantly engaged in different stages.</li>\n  <li><strong>Coercion and Punishment:</strong> The Gomasthas were outsiders with no social links to the villages. They acted arrogantly, marched into villages with <em>sepoys</em> and <em>peons</em>, and flogged and beat weavers for delays in supply. Prices given by the Company were pitiably low, driving many weavers to desert their villages and migrate.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Asserting monopoly right and eliminating rival European traders</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Role of Gomasthas as paid supervisory agents</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Advance loan trap binding weavers exclusively to the Company</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Abandonment of agricultural lands due to round-the-clock weaving</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Arrogance, physical punishment, and desertion of weaving villages</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s4q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s4q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why did industrial production in India increase during the First World War?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Industrial production in India expanded dramatically during the First World War (1914–1918) due to the following wartime developments:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Collapse of British Imports into India:</strong> With British mills busy with war production to meet the needs of the army, Manchester imports into India declined dramatically. Suddenly, Indian factories had a vast home market to supply without competition from foreign goods.</li>\n  <li><strong>War Orders from Colonial Government:</strong> As the war prolonged, Indian factories were called upon to supply war needs: jute bags, cloth for army uniforms, tents, leather boots, horse and mule saddles, and a host of other military equipment.</li>\n  <li><strong>Establishment of New Mills and Multiple Shifts:</strong> New factories were set up and old ones ran multiple shifts. Many new workers were employed and everyone was made to work longer hours. Over the war years, industrial production boomed.</li>\n  <li><strong>Permanent Loss of British Monopoly After the War:</strong> After the war, Manchester could never recapture its old position in the Indian market. Unable to modernise and compete with the US, Germany, and Japan, the economy of Britain crumbled. Within India, local industrialists consolidated their position, substituting foreign manufactures and capturing the domestic market.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Decline of Manchester imports creating vacant domestic market</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Government war contracts (jute bags, uniforms, tents, boots)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Multiple shifts, new factories, and expanded industrial workforce</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Post-war structural consolidation by Indian industrialists</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 4</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Case-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'When Indian manufacturers advertised, the nationalist message was clear and loud. If you care for the nation then buy products that are made in India. Advertisements became a vehicle of the nationalist message of swadeshi. Images of Indian gods and goddesses regularly appeared on calendars to make a divine appeal to consumers.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> How did marketing and advertisements help create a market for British and Indian goods in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Labels and Brand Credibility:</strong> British manufacturers put labels on cloth bundles (e.g., 'Made in Manchester') to ensure consumers trusted the quality. Images of gods (Krishna, Saraswati) were used on labels so that buyers felt a divine sanction when purchasing the cloth.</li><li><strong>Calendars in Common Households:</strong> Calendars were hung in tea shops and poor people's homes; unlike newspapers, even illiterate people saw these daily visual advertisements.</li><li><strong>Swadeshi Propaganda:</strong> Indian manufacturers used advertisements featuring nationalist leaders and the Bharat Mata figure to champion the Swadeshi movement, linking consumer choices directly with patriotism.</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> In the eighteenth century, the wages of workers in England were low and unemployment was widespread.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> The introduction of steam-powered machinery in all industries caused immediate displacement of the entire artisanal workforce.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> While workers faced extreme poverty, low wages, and seasonal unemployment, machines were NOT introduced in all industries immediately. Technological changes occurred slowly; machines were expensive, often broke down, and their repair was costly. Traditional hand-based methods dominated the vast majority of industries until well into the nineteenth century.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(3)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(5)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "5": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch5\" data-book=\"history\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">5</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">History: India and the Contemporary World – II</div>\n      <h2>Print Culture and the Modern World</h2>\n      <p>Unit 5: History • Gutenberg Press, Print Revolution, Reformation & Indian Nationalism — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s5q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s5q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Give reasons for the following: (a) Woodblock print only came to Europe after 1295, (b) Martin Luther was in favour of print and spoke out in praise of it, (c) The Roman Catholic Church began keeping an Index of Prohibited books from the mid-sixteenth century, (d) Gandhi said the fight for Swaraj is a fight for liberty of speech, liberty of the press, and freedom of association.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>(a) Woodblock print only came to Europe after 1295:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Woodblock printing was developed in China in the sixth century. In 1295, the great Italian explorer <strong>Marco Polo</strong> returned to Italy after many years of exploration in China.</li>\n  <li>Marco Polo brought this knowledge of woodblock printing back with him. Italians began producing books with woodblocks, and soon the technology spread to other parts of Europe.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(b) Martin Luther was in favour of print and spoke out in praise of it:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>In 1517, the religious reformer Martin Luther wrote <em>Ninety Five Theses</em> criticizing many of the practices and rituals of the Roman Catholic Church. A printed copy of this was pasted on a church door in Wittenberg.</li>\n  <li>Luther's writings were immediately reproduced in vast numbers and read widely. This led to a division within the Church and to the beginning of the <strong>Protestant Reformation</strong>. Luther’s translation of the New Testament sold 5,000 copies within a few weeks. Deeply moved, Luther declared: <em>'Print is the ultimate gift of God and the greatest one.'</em></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(c) The Roman Catholic Church began keeping an Index of Prohibited Books from the mid-sixteenth century:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Print and popular religious literature stimulated distinctive individual interpretations of faith even among little-educated people. In Italy, a miller named <strong>Menocchio</strong> read books available in his locality, reinterpreted the message of the Bible, and formulated a view of God and Creation that enraged the Catholic Church.</li>\n  <li>When the Roman Church began its Inquisitorial campaign to suppress heretical ideas, Menocchio was hauled up twice and ultimately executed. Troubled by such effects of popular readings and questionings of faith, the Roman Catholic Church imposed severe controls over publishers and booksellers and, from 1558, began maintaining an <strong>Index of Prohibited Books</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(d) Gandhi said the fight for Swaraj is a fight for liberty of speech, liberty of the press, and freedom of association:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Mahatma Gandhi believed that liberty of speech, liberty of the press, and freedom of association were the three most vital instruments of cultivating and mobilizing public opinion.</li>\n  <li>Colonial rulers sought to stifle these freedoms through censorship acts (such as the Vernacular Press Act) to deny Indians the right to criticize foreign exploitation. Gandhi argued that without these basic democratic liberties, no nation could assert self-determination or attain genuine Swaraj.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Marco Polo's return from China in 1295 with woodblock technology</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Martin Luther's Ninety Five Theses & Protestant Reformation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Menocchio's heresy, Inquisition, and Index of Prohibited Books 1558</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Gandhi's defense of civil liberties as core foundation of Swaraj</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s5q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s5q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Write short notes on: (a) The Gutenberg Press, (b) Erasmus's idea of the printed book, (c) The Vernacular Press Act.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>(a) The Gutenberg Press:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Inventor and Inspiration:</strong> Johannes Gutenberg developed the first known printing press at Strasbourg, Germany, in the 1430s. Drawing on his knowledge of wine presses, olive presses, and metal goldsmithing, he created metal types for each letter of the alphabet.</li>\n  <li><strong>First Printed Book:</strong> By 1448, Gutenberg perfected the movable type system. The first book he printed was the <strong>Bible</strong>. About 180 copies were printed, taking three years — which was remarkably fast for that era.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(b) Erasmus’s Idea of the Printed Book:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Latin Scholar and Catholic Reformer:</strong> Erasmus of Rotterdam expressed deep anxiety about the unregulated avalanche of printed books.</li>\n  <li><strong>Critique of Bad Books:</strong> In his book <em>Adages</em> (1508), he wrote that the world was being swamped with books that were ignorant, scandalous, irreligious, and seditious. He feared that the sheer volume of worthless publications eroded the value of valuable scholarship.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(c) The Vernacular Press Act (1878):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Modelled on Irish Press Laws:</strong> Passed by Lord Lytton's administration in 1878, it gave the colonial government extensive rights to censor reports and editorials in the vernacular press.</li>\n  <li><strong>Suppression of Nationalist Voice:</strong> When a report was judged seditious, the newspaper was warned, and if the warning was ignored, the press was liable to be seized and its printing machinery confiscated. Despite this, nationalist newspapers grew in numbers across India.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Gutenberg's invention, movable metal type & printing of the Bible</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Erasmus's critique of the flood of scandalous/irreligious books</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Vernacular Press Act 1878 provisions, censorship & confiscation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s5q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s5q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What did the spread of print culture in nineteenth-century India mean to: (a) Women, (b) The poor, (c) Reformers?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The spread of print culture in nineteenth-century India profoundly impacted different marginalized segments of society:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Impact on Women:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Women's reading increased enormously in middle-class homes. Liberal husbands and fathers began educating women at home and sent them to schools.</li>\n      <li>Women authors emerged: <strong>Rashsundari Debi</strong> in East Bengal secretly learned to read in the kitchen and wrote the first full-length autobiography in Bengali, <em>Amar Jiban</em> (1876). In Maharashtra, <strong>Kailashbashini Debi</strong>, <strong>Tarabai Shinde</strong>, and <strong>Pandita Ramabai</strong> wrote with passionate anger about the miserable plight of upper-caste Hindu widows, domestic oppression, and denial of education.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Impact on the Poor:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Very cheap small books were brought to markets in nineteenth-century Madras towns and sold at crossroads, allowing poor people travelling to markets to buy them.</li>\n      <li>Public libraries were set up from the early twentieth century, expanding access to books. Mill workers in Kanpur, Bombay, and Calcutta began writing and reading: <strong>Kashibaba</strong>, a Kanpur millworker, wrote <em>Chhote Aur Bade Ka Sawal</em> (1938) to expose links between caste and class exploitation.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Impact on Reformers:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Print became the primary vehicle for social and religious debate. Reformers used newspapers and tracts to attack orthodox practices like sati, child marriage, caste hierarchy, and idolatry.</li>\n      <li><strong>Raja Rammohun Roy</strong> published the <em>Sambad Kaumudi</em> from 1821, and the Hindu orthodoxy commissioned the <em>Samachar Chandrika</em> to oppose his ideas. Similarly, Urdu translations of scriptures and fatwas were printed in thousands by institutions like the Deoband Seminary to counter colonial Christian missionary influences.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Women: Literacy, Rashsundari Debi (Amar Jiban), Tarabai Shinde</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.75 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">The poor: Penny chapbooks, public libraries, Kanpur mill workers</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reformers: Rammohun Roy (Sambad Kaumudi), debate over social customs</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.75 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s5q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s5q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why did some people in eighteenth-century Europe think that print culture would bring enlightenment and end despotism?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>By the mid-eighteenth century, there was a common conviction that books were a means of spreading progress and enlightenment, as expressed by the French novelist <strong>Louis-Sébastien Mercier</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Spreading Reason and Rational Thinking:</strong> The writings of philosophers like Voltaire, Rousseau, and Montesquieu were widely printed and read. Their literature questioned traditional authority, religious dogma, and custom, arguing that everything ought to be judged through reason and rationality.</li>\n  <li><strong>Eroding the Legitimacy of Absolutist Monarchy:</strong> Print popularised the critical thinking that stripped the monarchy of its sacred aura. Cartoons and caricatures portrayed the monarchy as indulging in sensual pleasures while the common people suffered hardships, undermining public respect for despotism.</li>\n  <li><strong>Creation of a Public Sphere of Debate:</strong> Print created a new culture of dialogue and debate. All values, norms, and institutions were re-evaluated by a reading public that had become aware of the power of reason.</li>\n  <li><strong>Mercier’s Proclamation:</strong> Louis-Sébastien Mercier proclaimed: <em>'The printing press is the most powerful engine of progress and public opinion is the force that will sweep despotism away.'</em> In his novels, heroes are transformed by reading books. Mercier declared: <em>'Tremble, therefore, tyrants of the world! Tremble before the virtual writer!'</em></li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Enlightenment thinkers (Voltaire, Rousseau) and rational inquiry</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Satirical prints demystifying the royal authority</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Birth of a vibrant public sphere of critical debate</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mercier's famous quote on the printing press ending tyranny</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s5q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s5q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why did some people fear the effect of easily available printed books? Choose one example from Europe and one from India.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Many religious authorities, monarchs, and orthodox elites feared the spread of printed books because they worried that without control over what was published, rebellious and irreligious thoughts would gain currency:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Example from Europe (Menocchio and the Roman Catholic Church):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>In sixteenth-century Italy, a miller named <strong>Menocchio</strong> began reading books available in his locality. He reinterpreted the Bible and developed a theology that questioned the authority of the Catholic Church.</li>\n      <li>The Inquisition condemned him as a heretic and executed him. The Roman Church feared that print empowered commoners to question sacred scriptures, undermine clerical authority, and destroy the traditional social hierarchy, leading them to create the <em>Index of Prohibited Books</em> in 1558.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Example from India (Orthodox Hindu and Muslim Backlash):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>In India, conservative Hindus feared that educated women would be widowed, and conservative Muslims feared that educated women would be corrupted by reading romantic Urdu novels.</li>\n      <li>Orthodox Hindus worried that print would spread lower-caste ideas and challenge Brahminical dominance. Similarly, Islamic scholars (Ulama) feared that colonial rule would collapse Muslim personal laws, using lithographic presses to print thousands of fatwas telling Muslims how to conduct their lives.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Underlying fear: Subversion of traditional religious and political authority</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">European case study: Menocchio's interpretation, Inquisition & Index</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Indian case study: Fears of women's education, widowhood & caste upheaval</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s5q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s5q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain how print culture assisted the growth of nationalism in India.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Print culture was the primary catalyst in the growth of Indian nationalism during the nineteenth and twentieth centuries:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Exposing Colonial Exploitation:</strong> Despite repressive censorship laws, nationalist newspapers grew in all parts of India. They reported on colonial misrule, economic drainage, famine mismanagement, and racial arrogance, encouraging people to unite.</li>\n  <li><strong>Connecting Communities Across India:</strong> Print connected Indians living in different linguistic regions. A Gujarati paper discussed events in Bengal; a Tamil paper debated issues in Punjab. It forged a pan-Indian national consciousness.</li>\n  <li><strong>Propagating Nationalist Ideas:</strong> Prominent leaders published newspapers — Bal Gangadhar Tilak edited <em>Kesari</em> (Marathi) and <em>Mahratta</em> (English), while Gandhiji edited <em>Young India</em> and <em>Harijan</em>. Tilak’s reportage on the deportation of Punjab nationalists in 1907 led to his imprisonment in 1908, provoking widespread strikes across India.</li>\n  <li><strong>Revival of National Literature and Visuals:</strong> Nationalist songs like Bankim Chandra’s <em>Vande Mataram</em>, folk tales, and visual prints of Bharat Mata were printed and circulated by millions, giving Indians a profound sense of pride in their heritage.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reporting colonial economic exploitation and misgovernance</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Inter-regional connectivity creating pan-Indian consciousness</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Role of nationalist leaders & newspapers (Tilak's Kesari, Gandhi)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Dissemination of patriotic literature and Bharat Mata icons</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 5</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Case-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'From the late nineteenth century, issues of caste discrimination began to be written about in many printed tracts and essays. Jyotirao Phule, the Maratha pioneer of 'low caste' protest movements, wrote about the injustices of the caste system in his Gulamgiri (1871). In the twentieth century, B.R. Ambedkar in Maharashtra and E.V. Ramaswamy Naicker in Madras, better known as Periyar, wrote powerfully on caste and their writings were read by people all over India.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> How did print empower anti-caste movements in India?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Democratising Knowledge:</strong> Historically, lower castes were forbidden from reading sacred texts. Print demolished upper-caste monopolies over knowledge by producing accessible, inexpensive books in vernacular languages.</li><li><strong>Mobilising All-India Protest:</strong> Phule's <em>Gulamgiri</em>, Ambedkar's writings, and Periyar's radical critiques reached masses across provincial boundaries, sparking organized social movements against untouchability and caste dominance.</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> The Vernacular Press Act was passed by the British colonial government in 1878.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> The colonial government sought to suppress nationalist and anti-colonial criticism emerging in Indian language newspapers.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> As vernacular newspapers became assertively nationalist following the 1876–77 famines, Lord Lytton enacted the Vernacular Press Act, modeled on Irish coercion laws, giving magistrates the power to confiscate printing presses if seditious material was published against the British Crown.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(4)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(6)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "6": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch6\" data-book=\"geography\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">6</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Geography: Contemporary India – II</div>\n      <h2>Resources and Development</h2>\n      <p>Unit 6: Geography • Classification of Resources, Land Use, Soil Types & Conservation — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s6q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s6q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Answer the following in about 30 words: (i) Name three states having black soil and the crop mainly grown on it, (ii) What type of soil is found in the river deltas of the eastern coast? Give three main features of this type of soil, (iii) What steps can be taken to control soil erosion in the hilly areas? (iv) What are biotic and abiotic resources? Give some examples.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>(i) Black Soil Regions and Associated Major Crop:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>States:</strong> Maharashtra, Madhya Pradesh, Gujarat, and parts of Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh, and Chhattisgarh (covering the Deccan trap basalt region).</li>\n  <li><strong>Crop Grown:</strong> Cotton is the premier commercial crop grown on black soil (hence widely referred to as <em>Black Cotton Soil</em> or <em>Regur</em>). It is also ideal for sugarcane, jowar, wheat, and oilseeds.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(ii) Type of Soil in the River Deltas of the Eastern Coast (Three Main Features):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Soil Type:</strong> <strong>Alluvial Soil</strong> (deposited by the Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna, and Kaveri river systems).</li>\n  <li><strong>Feature 1 (High Fertility):</strong> Extremely fertile due to adequate proportions of potash, phosphoric acid, and lime, making it ideal for the growth of sugarcane, paddy, wheat, and cereal crops.</li>\n  <li><strong>Feature 2 (Classification by Age):</strong> Classified into older alluvium (<em>Bhangar</em>, containing higher concentration of calcareous <em>kankar</em> nodules) and newer alluvium (<em>Khadar</em>, finer particles, renewed annually by floods, and more fertile).</li>\n  <li><strong>Feature 3 (Intensive Cultivation):</strong> Because of its high fertility, regions of alluvial soils are intensively cultivated and densely populated.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(iii) Steps to Control Soil Erosion in Hilly Areas:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Contour Ploughing:</strong> Ploughing along the contour lines decelerates the flow of water down the slopes, preventing gully formation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Terrace Farming:</strong> Steps or terraces are cut out on slopes making step terraces to control runoff and prevent soil washaway (practised widely in Western and Central Himalayas).</li>\n  <li><strong>Strip Cropping:</strong> Large fields are divided into strips; strips of grass are left to grow between the crops to break up the kinetic force of the wind.</li>\n  <li><strong>Shelter Belts:</strong> Planting lines of trees to create shelter breaks wind velocity and stabilizes sand dunes and slopes.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>(iv) Biotic and Abiotic Resources (with Examples):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Biotic Resources:</strong> Resources obtained from the biosphere that possess life. <em>Examples:</em> Flora, fauna, human beings, fisheries, livestock, and forests.</li>\n  <li><strong>Abiotic Resources:</strong> All those things which are composed of non-living matter. <em>Examples:</em> Rocks, metals, land, water, air, and mineral ores.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Black soil states and cotton crop specification</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Alluvial soil deltas and 3 distinct pedological features</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Four hill soil erosion conservation methods</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Biotic vs Abiotic resources differentiation with examples</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s6q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s6q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain land use pattern in India and why the land under forest has not increased much since 1960-61.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Land Use Pattern in India:</strong></p>\n<p>India possesses a total geographical area of 3.28 million square kilometres. However, land use data is available for only 93% of the total area because land-use reporting for most of the north-eastern states (except Assam) has not been done fully, and some areas of Jammu & Kashmir and Ladakh occupied by Pakistan and China have not been surveyed.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Net Sown Area (NSA):</strong> Accounts for about 45.5% of total reporting area. The pattern of net sown area varies greatly from state to state: over 80% in Punjab and Haryana, but less than 10% in Arunachal Pradesh, Mizoram, Manipur, and Andaman & Nicobar Islands.</li>\n  <li><strong>Forest Cover:</strong> India's forest cover is roughly 24%, far lower than the essential <strong>33% of geographical area</strong> outlined in the National Forest Policy (1952) for ecological balance.</li>\n  <li><strong>Permanent Pastures:</strong> Land under permanent pasture has continually decreased, posing severe feed challenges for cattle populations.</li>\n  <li><strong>Waste Land and Non-Agricultural Uses:</strong> Rocky, arid, and desert areas, along with land put to non-agricultural uses like settlements, roads, railways, and industries.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>2. Reasons why Net Sown Area has not increased much:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Intense Pressure from Urbanisation and Infrastructure:</strong> Rapid industrialisation, urban expansion, highways, airports, and housing projects have encroached upon prime agricultural lands.</li>\n  <li><strong>Land Degradation:</strong> Continuous use of land over long periods without adequate conservation measures has resulted in severe land degradation (over 130 million hectares of degraded land in India: water-eroded, wind-eroded, and saline/alkaline).</li>\n  <li><strong>Over-Irrigation and Waterlogging:</strong> In Punjab, Haryana, and western UP, over-irrigation has caused waterlogging leading to increased salinity and alkalinity, rendering lands uncultivable.</li>\n  <li><strong>Depletion of Soil Fertility:</strong> Excessive application of chemical fertilisers, pesticides, and lack of crop rotation have exhausted the natural organic nutrients of the soil.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Geographical coverage and reporting statistics of land use in India</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Disparity in Net Sown Area across states (Punjab vs North-East)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Forest cover deficit compared to 33% National Forest Policy benchmark</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Four core reasons why NSA cannot be expanded further</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s6q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s6q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How have technical and economic development led to more consumption of resources?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Technical and economic development has directly accelerated the rate of resource consumption across the world and in India through the following mechanisms:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Technological Capability for Mass Exploitation:</strong> Modern technology provides sophisticated equipment, heavy earth-moving machinery, and deep-drilling tools that enable humans to extract minerals, petroleum, and groundwater at rates impossible in traditional societies.</li>\n  <li><strong>Industrialisation and Need for Raw Materials:</strong> Economic growth requires continuous expansion of manufacturing industries. Steel plants, thermal power stations, automobile units, and chemical factories devour vast quantities of coal, iron ore, limestone, water, and crude oil every day.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rising Living Standards and Consumerism:</strong> Economic development elevates per capita income and living standards. Affluent lifestyles lead to immense consumption of electricity, electronic gadgets, private motor vehicles, packaged foods, and luxury goods, multiplying resource throughput per person.</li>\n  <li><strong>Mechanisation of Agriculture:</strong> High-Yielding Variety (HYV) seeds, tractors, chemical fertilisers, and tube-wells have replaced traditional sustainable farming, depleting groundwater tables and fossil fuels.</li>\n  <li><strong>Urbanisation and Infrastructure Expansion:</strong> Concrete buildings, multi-lane expressways, bridges, and smart cities consume massive quantities of sand, gravel, water, cement, and metal ores.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Technological extraction capability outstripping natural replenishment</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Industrial expansion and raw material demands</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Affluent lifestyles, urbanization and consumer goods demand</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mechanised agriculture and water-table depletion</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Infrastructure development requiring massive mineral/metal inputs</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 6</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Case-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'At the international level, the Club of Rome advocated resource conservation for the first time in a more systematic way in 1968. Subsequently, in 1974, Gandhian philosophy was once again presented by Schumacher in his book Small is Beautiful. The seminal contribution with respect to resource conservation at the global level was made by the Brundtland Commission Report, 1987. This report introduced the concept of 'Sustainable Development' and advocated it as a means for resource conservation.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> What is 'Sustainable Development' and how does it balance economic growth with environmental conservation?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Definition:</strong> Sustainable economic development means <em>'development should take place without damaging the environment, and development in the present should not compromise with the needs of the future generations.'</em></li><li><strong>Practical Application:</strong> It requires replacing fossil fuels with renewable energy, practicing 3Rs (Reduce, Reuse, Recycle), controlling soil and water pollution, and ensuring equitable resource distribution so present economic advancement does not exhaust the planetary life-support system.</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> Arid soil develops on crystalline igneous rocks in areas of low rainfall.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> Arid soils range from red to brown in colour, are generally sandy in texture and saline in nature, and lack humus and moisture due to dry climate and high evaporation.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Assertion (A) is false, but Reason (R) is true.</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> Red and yellow soils develop on crystalline igneous rocks in areas of low rainfall (in the eastern and southern parts of the Deccan plateau). Arid soils develop in desert and dry regions like western Rajasthan due to intense weathering under desert conditions. Reason (R) accurately describes the characteristics of arid soil.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(5)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(7)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "7": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch7\" data-book=\"geography\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">7</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Geography: Contemporary India – II</div>\n      <h2>Forest and Wildlife Resources</h2>\n      <p>Unit 7: Geography • Biodiversity, IUCN Categories, Depletion Factors & Community Conservation — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s7q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s7q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Answer the following in about 30 words: (i) What is biodiversity? Why is biodiversity important for human lives? (ii) How have human activities affected the depletion of flora and fauna? Explain.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>(i) What is Biodiversity and Why is it Important for Human Lives?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Definition:</strong> Biodiversity (biological diversity) refers to the immensely rich variety of living organisms in an area, diverse in form and function, but closely integrated in a system through multiple networks of interdependencies.</li>\n  <li><strong>Importance to Human Lives:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Humans depend fundamentally on ecological systems for survival: plants, animals, and micro-organisms recreate the air we breathe, the water we drink, and the soil that produces our food.</li>\n      <li>Forests are the primary producers on which all other living beings depend; they provide medicinal plants, timber, resin, fuel, and regulate the global hydrological and carbon cycles.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(ii) How Human Activities Have Affected the Depletion of Flora and Fauna:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Agricultural Expansion:</strong> Between 1951 and 1980, according to the Forest Survey of India, over 26,200 sq. km. of forest area was converted into agricultural land all over India. Substantial parts of tribal belts, especially in the north-eastern and central India, have been deforested by shifting cultivation (<em>jhumming</em>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Large-scale River Valley Projects:</strong> Since 1951, over 5,000 sq. km. of forest was cleared for river valley projects (e.g., Narmada Sagar Project in Madhya Pradesh inundated 40,000 hectares of forest).</li>\n  <li><strong>Mining Activities:</strong> Mining causes severe deforestation. The Buxa Tiger Reserve in West Bengal is seriously threatened by ongoing dolomite mining.</li>\n  <li><strong>Habitat Destruction, Poaching and Hunting:</strong> Indiscriminate hunting of tigers, rhinos, and elephants for skins, horns, and tusks has pushed numerous species to the brink of extinction.</li>\n  <li><strong>Environmental Pollution and Forest Fires:</strong> Chemical poisoning, toxic agricultural runoff, and recurring forest fires destroy micro-habitats and decimate wildlife populations.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition of biodiversity and ecological web of interdependence</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Significance to human survival (air, water, soil renewal, medicine)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Agricultural expansion & Jhum cultivation impact</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mining, river dams, and poaching impacts on fauna</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s7q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s7q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Describe how communities have conserved and protected forests and wildlife in India.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>In India, traditional local communities have played an inspiring and heroic role in conserving forests and wildlife:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Sariska Tiger Reserve, Rajasthan:</strong> In Sariska, villagers have fought against mining by citing the Wildlife Protection Act, successfully preventing commercial exploitation of the tiger sanctuary.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bhairodev Dakav 'Sonchuri' (Alwar, Rajasthan):</strong> The inhabitants of five villages in the Alwar district of Rajasthan have declared 1,200 hectares of forest as the <em>Bhairodev Dakav 'Sonchuri'</em>, framing their own set of rules and regulations which do not allow hunting and protect the wildlife against any outside encroachments.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Famous Chipko Movement (Uttarakhand):</strong> The historic Chipko Movement in the Himalayas successfully resisted deforestation in several areas and demonstrated that community afforestation with indigenous species can be enormously successful.</li>\n  <li><strong>Beej Bachao Andolan and Navdanya:</strong> Farmers and citizen groups like the <em>Beej Bachao Andolan</em> in Tehri and <em>Navdanya</em> have shown that adequate levels of diversified crop production without the use of synthetic chemicals are possible and economically viable.</li>\n  <li><strong>Joint Forest Management (JFM) Programme:</strong> JFM furnishes a formal institutional framework where local village communities collaborate with state forest departments to protect degraded forests (first formal resolution passed by Odisha in 1988). In return, communities are entitled to intermediary benefits like non-timber forest produce and a share in the timber harvested.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sacred Groves (Bhairon, Deorais):</strong> Nature worship is an age-old tribal belief. Sacred groves preserved untouched by local communities are found in Meghalaya (Khasi and Jaintia Hills), Western Ghats, and Aravalli Hills of Rajasthan.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Sariska & Alwar Bhairodev Dakav Sonchuri community initiative</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Chipko movement resisting Himalayan tree felling</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Beej Bachao Andolan & organic seed conservation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Joint Forest Management (JFM) institutional model and benefits</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Sacred groves and traditional nature worship practices</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s7q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s7q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Write a detailed note on the classification of forests in India: Reserved, Protected, and Unclassed Forests.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Government Administrative Classification of Forests in India:</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;margin-top:10px;\">\n  <tr style=\"background:#f0fdfa;color:#115e59;\">\n    <th>Forest Category</th>\n    <th>Legal Status &amp; Degree of Protection</th>\n    <th>Distribution Across States</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Reserved Forests</strong></td>\n    <td>Regarded as the most valuable as far as the conservation of forest and wildlife resources are concerned. More than half of the total forest land has been declared reserved. Strict prohibition on grazing, hunting, and timber felling.</td>\n    <td>Jammu &amp; Kashmir, Andhra Pradesh, Uttarakhand, Kerala, Tamil Nadu, West Bengal, and Maharashtra have large percentages of reserved forests.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Protected Forests</strong></td>\n    <td>Almost one-third of the total forest area is protected forest as declared by the Forest Department. This forest land is protected from any further depletion; local communities are often allowed limited grazing and firewood collection rights without causing damage.</td>\n    <td>Bihar, Haryana, Punjab, Himachal Pradesh, Odisha, and Rajasthan have a bulk of their forests under protected category.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Unclassed Forests</strong></td>\n    <td>These are other forests and wastelands belonging to both government and private individuals and communities. Communities manage them locally with customary rights.</td>\n    <td>All North-Eastern states and parts of Gujarat have a very high percentage of unclassed forests managed by local communities.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reserved forests definition, percentage (>50%), and state examples</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Protected forests definition (~33%), conservation rules, and states</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Unclassed forests definition, community ownership, and North-East dominance</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 7</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Case-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'Project Tiger, one of the well-publicised wildlife campaigns in the world, was launched in 1973. Tiger population had dwindled to 1,827 from an estimated 55,000 at the turn of the century. The major threats to tiger population are numerous, such as poaching for trade, shrinking habitat, depletion of prey base species, and growing human population.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> Why is Project Tiger viewed not just as an effort to save an endangered species, but as an exercise of great ecological magnitude?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Apex Predator and Ecological Health:</strong> The tiger is an apex predator at the top of the food chain. Conserving the tiger necessitates preserving the entire ecosystem — protecting vast forest habitats, herbivores (deer, wild boar), flora, and water catchments.</li><li><strong>Prominent Tiger Reserves:</strong> Corbett National Park (Uttarakhand), Sundarbans National Park (West Bengal), Bandhavgarh National Park (Madhya Pradesh), Sariska Wildlife Sanctuary (Rajasthan), Manas Tiger Reserve (Assam), and Periyar Tiger Reserve (Kerala) serve as crucial biodiversity repositories.</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> The Himalayan Yew is a medicinal plant found in various parts of Himachal Pradesh and Arunachal Pradesh.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> A chemical compound called 'taxol' is extracted from the bark, needles, twigs and roots of this tree, and it has been successfully used to treat cancer — the drug is now the biggest-selling anti-cancer drug in the world.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> Due to excessive commercial over-exploitation for taxol production over the last decade, thousands of yew trees have dried up in various parts of Himachal Pradesh and Arunachal Pradesh, putting the species under severe threat of extinction.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(6)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(8)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "8": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch8\" data-book=\"geography\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">8</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Geography: Contemporary India – II</div>\n      <h2>Water Resources</h2>\n      <p>Unit 8: Geography • Water Scarcity, Multi-purpose Projects & Rainwater Harvesting — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s8q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s8q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Answer the following in about 30 words: (i) Explain how water becomes a renewable resource, (ii) What is water scarcity and what are its main causes? (iii) Compare the advantages and disadvantages of multi-purpose river projects.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>(i) How Water Becomes a Renewable Resource:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Water is a renewable resource because it is continuously recycled, purified, and recharged through the <strong>hydrological cycle</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Water evaporates from oceans and water bodies due to solar heat, condenses to form clouds in the atmosphere, and precipitates back to Earth as rain and snow. It flows through rivers, infiltrates underground aquifers, and returns to the oceans, maintaining a continuous perpetual cycle.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(ii) What is Water Scarcity and What are its Main Causes?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Water Scarcity:</strong> A situation where water availability is insufficient to meet the basic drinking, sanitation, and economic demands of a region's population.</li>\n  <li><strong>Main Causes:</strong>\n    <ol>\n      <li><em>Rapid Population Growth:</em> Growing population requires more water for domestic use and to produce more food grains.</li>\n      <li><em>Expansion of Irrigated Agriculture:</em> Water-intensive cash crops and tube-wells have caused catastrophic depletion of underground aquifers.</li>\n      <li><em>Industrialisation and Urbanisation:</em> Industries are heavy consumers of water and hydroelectric power, while urban housing complexes draw massive quantities of groundwater using personal submersible pumps.</li>\n      <li><em>Water Pollution:</em> Even where water is amply available, it is often contaminated by domestic and industrial wastes, chemicals, pesticides, and fertilisers, making it hazardous for human use.</li>\n    </ol>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(iii) Compare the Advantages and Disadvantages of Multi-purpose River Projects:</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;margin-top:8px;\">\n  <tr style=\"background:#f0fdfa;color:#115e59;\">\n    <th>Advantages (Merits)</th>\n    <th>Disadvantages (Demerits)</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Generation of clean, renewable hydroelectric power.</td>\n    <td>1. Regulating and damming rivers affects natural flow, causing excessive sedimentation at the reservoir bottom.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Irrigation facilities to drought-prone agricultural regions.</td>\n    <td>2. Submergence of fertile agricultural lands and valuable natural forest ecosystems.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Flood control during monsoon surges.</td>\n    <td>3. Large-scale displacement of local indigenous populations without proper rehabilitation.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. Inland navigation and recreational fisheries.</td>\n    <td>4. Induces seismicity (earthquakes) and triggers water-borne diseases and pests.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Hydrological cycle mechanism ensuring renewability</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Water scarcity definition and 4 causes (population, irrigation, pollution)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Advantages of multi-purpose river projects (power, irrigation, flood control)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Disadvantages (sedimentation, displacement, submergence, earthquakes)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s8q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s8q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Discuss how rainwater harvesting in semi-arid regions of Rajasthan is carried out.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>In the semi-arid and arid regions of Rajasthan (particularly Bikaner, Phalodi, and Barmer), rainwater harvesting was traditionally carried out through an ingenious underground structure known as a <strong>'Tanka'</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Structure and Capacity:</strong> The tankas were large underground tanks built inside the main house or the courtyard. They could be as large as a big room (e.g., a tanka in Phalodi was 6.1 metres deep, 4.27 metres long, and 2.44 metres wide).</li>\n  <li><strong>Rooftop Catchment System:</strong> The tankas were part of the well-developed rooftop rainwater harvesting system. Sloping roofs of the houses were connected to the underground tanka through a pipe. Rain falling on the rooftops would travel down the pipe and get stored in the underground tankas.</li>\n  <li><strong>Filtration of First Rain:</strong> The first spell of rain was usually not collected as this would clean the roof and the pipes. The rainwater from subsequent showers was then collected.</li>\n  <li><strong>Reliable Year-round Drinking Water:</strong> The water collected in these tankas was called <em>Palar Pani</em>, considered the purest form of natural water. It was sealed and kept cool underground, providing sweet drinking water through the scorching summer months when all other sources dried up.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cooling the Household:</strong> Many houses built rooms adjoining the tanka to beat the desert summer heat, as it kept the room pleasantly cool.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Tanka structure, dimensions and courtyard positioning</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Sloping roof and pipe connectivity system</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Exclusion of first rain and storage of subsequent clean rain</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Palar Pani concept, drinking water reliability & cooling effect</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s8q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s8q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Describe how modern adaptations of traditional rainwater harvesting methods are being carried out to conserve and store water.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Modern adaptations of traditional rainwater harvesting are being actively implemented across urban and rural India to combat water crises:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Rooftop Rainwater Harvesting in Urban Areas:</strong> Modern residential colonies, apartment complexes, and schools install PVC pipes connecting rooftop runoff directly through a filtration pit (filled with sand and gravel) into underground storage tanks or recharge borewells, directly replenishing the city's water table.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tamil Nadu Model (Mandatory Legal Framework):</strong> Tamil Nadu is the first state in India which has made rooftop rainwater harvesting structure compulsory to all houses across the state. There are legal provisions to punish the defaulters. This has resulted in a spectacular recharge of groundwater levels across Chennai and rural districts.</li>\n  <li><strong>Gendathur Village, Karnataka (Rural Success Story):</strong> In Gendathur, a remote backward village in Mysuru, Karnataka, villagers installed rooftop rainwater harvesting systems in nearly 200 households. With an annual precipitation of 1,000 mm and 80% collection efficiency, the village harvests nearly 1,00,000 litres of water annually per household, earning the distinction of being rich in rainwater.</li>\n  <li><strong>Modern Check Dams and Johads in Rural Watersheds:</strong> Traditional <em>Johads</em> and <em>Khadins</em> in Rajasthan have been revived by NGOs (like Tarun Bharat Sangh headed by Rajendra Singh) using modern engineering to check soil erosion and recharge dry borewells.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bamboo Drip Irrigation in Meghalaya:</strong> In Meghalaya, a 200-year-old system of tapping stream and spring water by using bamboo pipes is still adapted to irrigate betel leaf crops on steep slopes without water wastage.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Urban rooftop filtration pit and aquifer recharge technique</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Tamil Nadu legal mandate making rainwater harvesting compulsory</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Gendathur village (Mysuru) model harvesting 1 lakh litres/household</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Revival of Johads/Khadins via watershed check dams</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Bamboo drip irrigation in Meghalaya for hill slope conservation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 8</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Case-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'Jawaharlal Nehru proudly proclaimed the dams as the 'temples of modern India'; the reason being that it would integrate development of agriculture and the village economy with rapid industrialisation and growth of the urban economy. However, in recent years, multi-purpose projects and large dams have come under great scrutiny and opposition from environmental and human rights groups.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> Why have social movements like 'Narmada Bachao Andolan' and 'Tehri Dam Andolan' emerged against multi-purpose river projects?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Massive Involuntary Displacement:</strong> Building large dams submerges vast areas of ancestral land and forests, forcibly uprooting tribal and rural communities from their livelihoods without adequate rehabilitation.</li><li><strong>Ecological Destruction and Siltation:</strong> Damming disrupts natural river ecology, prevents fish migration, causes excessive reservoir sedimentation, waterlogging, and increases the salinity of surrounding agricultural soil.</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> Rainwater, or 'Palar Pani', as commonly known in parts of Rajasthan, is considered the purest form of natural water.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> Rainwater collected directly from clean rooftops and stored in dark, underground, sealed tankas is completely protected from sunlight, air pollutants, and bacterial decay.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> The traditional wisdom of building underground tankas in western Rajasthan ensured that rainwater remained clean, sweet, and uncontaminated for months, serving as an indispensable lifeline during harsh desert summers.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(7)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(9)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "9": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch9\" data-book=\"geography\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">9</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Geography: Contemporary India – II</div>\n      <h2>Agriculture</h2>\n      <p>Unit 9: Geography • Cropping Seasons, Major Food & Cash Crops, Technical Reforms — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s9q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s9q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Answer the following in about 30 words: (i) Name one important beverage crop and specify the geographical conditions required for its growth, (ii) Name one staple crop of India and the regions where it is produced, (iii) Enlist the various institutional reform programmes introduced by the government in the interest of farmers.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>(i) Important Beverage Crop and Geographical Conditions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Beverage Crop:</strong> <strong>Tea</strong> (or Coffee).</li>\n  <li><strong>Geographical Conditions for Tea:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Climate:</em> Tropical and sub-tropical climate with warm and moist frost-free climate throughout the year.</li>\n      <li><em>Soil:</em> Deep, fertile, well-drained soil, rich in humus and organic matter.</li>\n      <li><em>Rainfall:</em> Frequent showers evenly distributed throughout the year (150 to 200 cm) ensure continuous growth of tender green leaves.</li>\n      <li><em>Topography:</em> Gently rolling hill slopes to prevent waterlogging around roots. Major producing states: Assam, West Bengal (Darjeeling hills), Tamil Nadu, and Kerala.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(ii) Staple Crop of India and Major Producing Regions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Staple Crop:</strong> <strong>Rice</strong> (consumed by the majority of people in India).</li>\n  <li><strong>Producing Regions:</strong> Plains of north and north-eastern India, coastal areas, and the deltaic regions (West Bengal, Uttar Pradesh, Punjab, Andhra Pradesh, Bihar, Odisha, and Tamil Nadu). Development of canal irrigation and tube-wells has made it possible to grow rice even in low rainfall areas of Punjab, Haryana, and western Uttar Pradesh.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(iii) Institutional Reform Programmes Introduced by the Government for Farmers:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Collectivisation and Consolidation of Land Holdings:</strong> Consolidation of small fragmented holdings (<em>chakbandi</em>) and abolition of the exploitative <em>zamindari</em> system.</li>\n  <li><strong>Crop Insurance:</strong> Provision for crop insurance against drought, flood, cyclone, fire, and disease under the <em>Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Institutional Credit (KCC &amp; PAIS):</strong> Establishment of Grameen banks, cooperative societies, and banks to provide loans at low interest rates; introduction of <strong>Kisan Credit Card (KCC)</strong> and <strong>Personal Accident Insurance Scheme (PAIS)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Minimum Support Price (MSP):</strong> Announcement of Minimum Support Price (MSP), remunerative and procurement prices for important crops to check the exploitation of farmers by speculators and middlemen.</li>\n  <li><strong>Weather Bulletins and Agri-programmes:</strong> Special weather bulletins and agricultural programmes for farmers broadcast on radio and television.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Tea beverage crop geographical requirements (temperature, soil, rainfall)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Rice staple crop identification and major producing regions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Five institutional reforms (Consolidation, KCC, Crop Insurance, MSP)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s9q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s9q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Describe the geographical conditions required for the growth of rice.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Geographical Requirements for the Cultivation of Rice in India:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Crop Type and Status:</strong> Rice is a <em>Kharif</em> crop and the staple food grain of the majority of the population in India. India is the second-largest producer of rice in the world after China.</li>\n  <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> Requires high temperature (above <strong>25°C</strong>) during growing and harvesting periods.</li>\n  <li><strong>Humidity and Rainfall:</strong> High humidity with annual rainfall above <strong>100 cm</strong>. In regions with less rainfall (like Punjab, Haryana, western UP, and parts of Rajasthan), it is cultivated successfully with the help of canal irrigation and tube-wells.</li>\n  <li><strong>Soil Type:</strong> Deep clayey and loamy alluvial soils that have high water-retention capacity are best suited for rice cultivation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Labour:</strong> Requires abundant cheap and skilled labour, as most of the operations like transplanting seedlings, weeding, and harvesting are performed manually by hand.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Kharif crop classification and national staple importance</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">High temperature requirement (>25°C)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">High rainfall requirement (>100 cm) and canal irrigation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Clayey alluvial water-retentive soil</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Labour-intensive farming requirement</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s9q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s9q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Describe the impact of globalisation on Indian agriculture.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Globalisation has exposed Indian agriculture to both significant opportunities and severe structural challenges, particularly since 1991:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Exposure to Unfair Global Competition:</strong> Under globalisation, despite being an important producer of rice, cotton, rubber, tea, coffee, jute, and spices, Indian agricultural products are unable to compete with developed countries. This is because agriculture in developed nations (like the US and EU) is heavily subsidised by their governments.</li>\n  <li><strong>Agrarian Distress and Farmers' Suicides:</strong> Rising cost of production (expensive seeds, pesticides, and electricity) coupled with declining subsidies on fertilisers has reduced farm profitability, leading to indebtedness and agrarian distress in several states (e.g., Maharashtra, Andhra Pradesh, Punjab).</li>\n  <li><strong>Genetic Modification and Gene Revolution:</strong> Modern biotechnology and Genetically Modified (GM) seeds (like Bt Cotton) have increased yields, but have made farmers heavily dependent on private multinational seed corporations.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rise of Organic Farming:</strong> Globalisation has created strong international demand for certified organic agricultural produce grown without chemical fertilisers or pesticides, offering lucrative export avenues for Indian organic cultivators.</li>\n  <li><strong>Need for Diversification towards High-Value Crops:</strong> Economists suggest that Indian farmers should diversify their cropping pattern from cereals to high-value crops (fruits, vegetables, medicinal herbs, flowers, and bio-diesel crops like jatropha and jojoba). This will increase incomes and reduce environmental degradation simultaneously, as fruits and herbs require much less water than sugarcane or paddy.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Unequal competition with heavily subsidised Western agriculture</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Agrarian distress, input cost escalation and subsidy cuts</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Impact of GM seeds and corporate dependency</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Organic farming opportunities and high-value crop diversification</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 9</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Case-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'Vinoba Bhave undertook padyatra to spread Gandhiji's message across India. Once, at Pochampalli in Andhra Pradesh, some landless poor villagers demanded 80 acres of land for their economic survival. Suddenly, Shri Ram Chandra Reddy stood up and offered 80 acres of land to be distributed among 80 landless villagers. This act was known as 'Bhoodan'. Later, he travelled and introduced his ideas throughout India, and some zamindars offered to distribute some villages among the landless. This was known as 'Gramdan'.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> Why is the Bhoodan-Gramdan movement initiated by Vinoba Bhave referred to as the 'Blood-less Revolution'?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Voluntary Land Redistribution:</strong> Unlike violent communist agrarian revolutions that forcefully seized estates through bloodshed, Vinoba Bhave appealed peacefully to the conscience and moral duty of wealthy landlords.</li><li><strong>Peaceful Gandhian Transformation:</strong> Landowners voluntarily gifted lakhs of acres of fertile land (Bhoodan) and entire villages (Gramdan) to landless peasants without any state coercion or violent conflict, making it a unique 'Blood-less Revolution'.</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> Intensive subsistence farming is practised in areas of high population pressure on land.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> High doses of biochemical inputs and irrigation are used for obtaining higher production from limited land due to the right of inheritance leading to fragmentation of holdings.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> Successive division of agricultural land among generations has reduced farm plot sizes to an uneconomical level. Farmers have no alternative source of livelihood, so they apply maximum possible labour, fertiliser, and irrigation to squeeze maximum output from their tiny plots.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(8)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(10)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "10": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch10\" data-book=\"geography\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">10</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Geography: Contemporary India – II</div>\n      <h2>Minerals and Energy Resources</h2>\n      <p>Unit 10: Geography • Ferrous & Non-Ferrous Minerals, Conventional & Non-Conventional Energy — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s10q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s10q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Answer the following in about 30 words: (i) Distinguish between ferrous and non-ferrous minerals, (ii) What is a mineral? (iii) How are minerals formed in igneous and metamorphic rocks? (iv) Why do we need to conserve mineral resources?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>(i) Distinguish between Ferrous and Non-Ferrous Minerals:</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;\">\n  <tr style=\"background:#f0fdfa;color:#115e59;\">\n    <th>Feature</th>\n    <th>Ferrous Minerals</th>\n    <th>Non-Ferrous Minerals</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Iron Content</strong></td>\n    <td>Metallic minerals that contain iron.</td>\n    <td>Metallic minerals that do not contain iron.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Economic Share</strong></td>\n    <td>Account for about three-fourths of the total value of production of metallic minerals in India.</td>\n    <td>Reserves and production are comparatively less satisfactory in India, but crucial for metallurgy and electronics.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Examples</strong></td>\n    <td>Iron ore, Manganese, Chromite, Nickel, Cobalt.</td>\n    <td>Copper, Bauxite (Aluminium), Lead, Zinc, Gold.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n\n<p><strong>(ii) What is a Mineral?</strong></p>\n<p>Geologists define a mineral as a <em>'homogenous, naturally occurring substance with a definable internal crystalline structure.'</em> Minerals are found in varied forms in nature, ranging from the hardest diamond to the softest talc.</p>\n\n<p><strong>(iii) How are Minerals Formed in Igneous and Metamorphic Rocks?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>In igneous and metamorphic rocks, minerals may occur in the cracks, crevices, faults, or joints. The smaller occurrences are called <strong>veins</strong> and the larger are called <strong>lodes</strong>.</li>\n  <li>In most cases, they are formed when minerals in liquid/molten and gaseous forms are forced upward through cavities towards the earth’s surface. They cool and solidify as they rise. Major metallic minerals like tin, copper, zinc, and lead are obtained from veins and lodes.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(iv) Why do we need to Conserve Mineral Resources?</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Finite and Non-Renewable:</strong> Mineral formation requires millions of years of geological processes; the present rate of consumption vastly exceeds the infinitesimal rate of geological replenishment.</li>\n  <li><strong>Decreasing Quality and Increasing Cost:</strong> Continued extraction leads to depletion of high-grade ores, forcing mining at greater depths accompanied by lower grades and escalated extraction costs.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ferrous vs Non-ferrous minerals distinction with examples</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Scientific definition of a mineral</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mode of occurrence in igneous/metamorphic rocks (veins and lodes)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Need for conservation (finite nature, non-renewability, rising costs)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s10q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s10q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Describe the distribution of coal in India.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Coal is the most abundantly available fossil fuel in India, providing a substantial part of the nation's energy needs. In India, coal occurs in rock sequences of two main geological ages:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Gondwana Coal Deposits (Over 200 Million Years Old):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Constitutes the vast bulk of India’s metallurgical and commercial coal reserves (bituminous coal).</li>\n      <li><em>Damodar Valley Basin (West Bengal–Jharkhand):</em> The premier coalfields of India are located here: <strong>Jharia</strong> (richest coking coal), <strong>Raniganj</strong> (oldest coalfield), <strong>Bokaro</strong>, Giridih, and Karanpura.</li>\n      <li><em>Other River Valleys:</em> Godavari valley (Singareni in Telangana), Mahanadi valley (Talcher and Ib river in Odisha), and Wardha-Son valleys (Singrauli and Chanda in MP/Maharashtra).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Tertiary Coal Deposits (Only about 55 Million Years Old):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Younger, softer coal with higher moisture and sulfur content.</li>\n      <li>Found extensively in the north-eastern states, namely <strong>Meghalaya</strong> (Darangiri, Cherrapunji), <strong>Assam</strong> (Makum, Nazira), <strong>Arunachal Pradesh</strong> (Namchik-Namphuk), and <strong>Nagaland</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Lignite Coal Deposits:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Low-grade brown coal, soft with high moisture content. The principal lignite reserves are located at <strong>Neyveli in Tamil Nadu</strong>, used predominantly for thermal power generation.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Gondwana coal age (>200 million years) and characteristics</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Damodar Valley coalfields (Jharia, Raniganj, Bokaro)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Other major Gondwana basins (Godavari, Mahanadi, Son)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Tertiary coal in North-Eastern states (Assam, Meghalaya, Arunachal)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Neyveli lignite deposits in Tamil Nadu</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">0.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s10q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s10q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why do you think that solar energy has a bright future in India?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Solar energy has an exceptionally bright and transformative future in India due to the following strategic, geographical, and environmental factors:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Favourable Tropical Location:</strong> India is a tropical country endowed with enormous solar radiation. It receives about 300 clear sunny days a year, with an estimated solar energy potential of about 20 MW per square kilometre per annum.</li>\n  <li><strong>Vast Potential in Arid and Semi-Arid Regions:</strong> Western India, particularly Rajasthan (Thar Desert) and Gujarat, has vast barren tracts with abundant sunshine, making them ideal for massive solar photovoltaic and concentrated solar power parks (e.g., Bhadla Solar Park in Rajasthan, one of the world's largest).</li>\n  <li><strong>Cost-Effective Photovoltaic Technology:</strong> Photovoltaic technology converts sunlight directly into electricity. With rapid technological advances and economies of scale, solar tariff costs have plummeted drastically, making solar cheaper than conventional thermal power.</li>\n  <li><strong>Decentralised Power for Rural India:</strong> Solar energy can be set up in decentralized units (solar pumps, rooftop panels, solar lanterns) in remote villages where setting up high-voltage grid lines is uneconomic. This reduces rural dependence on firewood and dung cakes, conserving forests and cattle manure for agriculture.</li>\n  <li><strong>Environmental Sustainability:</strong> Solar power is 100% clean, renewable, and emits zero greenhouse gases, directly helping India fulfill its international climate commitments (Panchamrit goals / Net Zero by 2070).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Tropical geography & 300 sunny days per year</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Immense potential in Rajasthan/Gujarat barren desert regions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Affordable photovoltaic technology and tumbling costs</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Decentralized rural electrification replacing firewood & dung cakes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Clean, non-polluting energy helping achieve Net Zero targets</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 10</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Case-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'In Meghalaya, there are large deposits of coal, iron ore, limestone and dolomite etc. Coal mining in Jowai and Cherrapunji is done by family members in the form of a long narrow tunnel, known as a 'Rat hole' mining. The National Green Tribunal (NGT) has declared such activities illegal and recommended that these should be stopped forthwith.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> What is 'Rat-hole mining' and why has it been declared hazardous and illegal by the National Green Tribunal?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Method:</strong> In Meghalaya, mineral ownership vests with tribal individuals or communities. Primitive miners dig vertical pits and crawl through dangerously narrow horizontal tunnels (like rat holes) to extract coal.</li><li><strong>Hazards:</strong> It lacks basic safety equipment; during monsoons, tunnels flood instantly, drowning workers (frequently child labourers). Furthermore, it causes severe acid mine drainage, polluting surrounding rivers like the Lukha and ruining drinking water.</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> Natural gas is considered an environment-friendly fuel.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> Natural gas produces low carbon dioxide emissions on combustion and leaves behind no ash or toxic residues, making it a key transition fuel.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> Natural gas consists primarily of methane. Compared to coal and petroleum, it emits substantially less carbon monoxide, sulfur dioxide, and particulate matter, making Compressed Natural Gas (CNG) and Piped Natural Gas (PNG) the fuel of choice for urban transport and households.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(9)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(11)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "11": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch11\" data-book=\"geography\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">11</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Geography: Contemporary India – II</div>\n      <h2>Manufacturing Industries</h2>\n      <p>Unit 11: Geography • Industrial Location, Agro vs Mineral Industries, Pollution Control — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s11q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s11q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Answer the following in about 30 words: (i) What is manufacturing? (ii) Name any three physical and three human factors for the location of an industry, (iii) What are basic industries? Give an example, (iv) Name the important raw materials used in the manufacturing of cement.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>(i) What is Manufacturing?</strong></p>\n<p>Production of goods in large quantities after processing from raw materials to more valuable products is called <strong>manufacturing</strong> (e.g., paper from wood, sugar from sugarcane, iron and steel from iron ore, and aluminium from bauxite). It belongs to the <strong>secondary sector</strong> of the economy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>(ii) Factors Influencing Industrial Location:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Three Physical Factors:</strong>\n    <ol>\n      <li>Availability of abundant raw materials near the factory site.</li>\n      <li>Perennial supply of cheap water and continuous power/energy.</li>\n      <li>Favourable climate and suitable topography.</li>\n    </ol>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Three Human/Socio-Economic Factors:</strong>\n    <ol>\n      <li>Availability of cheap, skilled and unskilled labour.</li>\n      <li>Proximity to markets and efficient transport networks.</li>\n      <li>Access to banking, insurance, capital, and supportive government industrial policies.</li>\n    </ol>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(iii) What are Basic Industries? Give an Example:</strong></p>\n<p>Basic or key industries are those which supply their products or raw materials to manufacture other goods. <em>Example:</em> <strong>Iron and steel industry</strong>, copper smelting, and aluminium smelting. Machinery, transport equipment, and consumer tools all rely on basic steel.</p>\n\n<p><strong>(iv) Important Raw Materials Used in Manufacturing Cement:</strong></p>\n<p>Limestone, silica, alumina, and gypsum, along with coal and electric power.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition of manufacturing and secondary sector role</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Three physical and three human location factors</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Basic industries definition and iron & steel example</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Raw materials for cement (limestone, silica, alumina, gypsum)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s11q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s11q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How do industries pollute the environment? Explain the four major types of industrial pollution.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Industries are responsible for four major types of environmental pollution:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Air Pollution:</strong> Caused by the presence of high proportion of undesirable gases like sulfur dioxide and carbon monoxide. Smoke is emitted by chemical and paper factories, brick kilns, refineries, and thermal power plants. Toxic gas leaks (e.g., Bhopal Gas Tragedy 1984) are hazardous to human health, flora, and fauna.</li>\n  <li><strong>Water Pollution:</strong> Caused by organic and inorganic industrial wastes and effluents discharged into freshwater bodies. The main culprits are paper, pulp, chemical, textile, dyeing, tanneries, petroleum refineries, and electroplating industries. They dump heavy metals like lead, mercury, pesticides, fertilisers, synthetic chemicals, and plastics into rivers.</li>\n  <li><strong>Thermal Pollution:</strong> Occurs when hot water from factories and thermal plants is drained into rivers and ponds before cooling. It drastically lowers dissolved oxygen, destroying aquatic life, fish eggs, and aquatic plants.</li>\n  <li><strong>Noise Pollution:</strong> Industrial and construction activities, machinery, generators, saws, and pneumatic drills create unwanted high decibel sounds, causing hearing impairment, hypertension, stress, and physiological disorders among workers.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Air pollution: Toxic gas emissions and industrial smoke sources</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Water pollution: Industrial chemical effluents & heavy metal dumping</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Thermal pollution: Discharge of hot water harming aquatic life</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Noise pollution: Industrial machinery noise and health impacts</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s11q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s11q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Discuss the steps to be taken to minimise environmental degradation by industry.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>To achieve sustainable industrial growth, environmental degradation caused by industries can be effectively controlled through the following concrete measures:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Minimising Water Use and Recycling:</strong> Minimising water consumption by reusing and recycling it in two or more successive stages. Harvesting rainwater to meet industrial water requirements.</li>\n  <li><strong>Three-stage Treatment of Industrial Effluents:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Primary Treatment:</em> By mechanical means — screening, sedimentation, and flocculation.</li>\n      <li><em>Secondary Treatment:</em> By biological processes (aeration and biological filters).</li>\n      <li><em>Tertiary Treatment:</em> By biological, chemical, and physical processes including recycling of wastewater.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Control of Air Pollution:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Fitting smoke stacks with electrostatic precipitators, fabric filters, scrubbers, and inertial separators to trap particulate matter before smoke is released.</li>\n      <li>Replacing coal with oil or natural gas in industrial furnaces to eliminate black soot emissions.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Control of Noise Pollution:</strong> Machinery and equipment can be fitted with silencers. Redesigning machinery to make it energy-efficient and quiet; workers should wear earplugs and earphones.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ecological Zoning and Green Belts:</strong> Developing green belts of dense tree plantations around industrial complexes to absorb sound, dust, and toxic fumes.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Water minimization, reuse and rainwater harvesting</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Three-stage effluent treatment (Primary, Secondary, Tertiary)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Air pollution controls: Electrostatic precipitators, scrubbers, gas fuel</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Noise reduction equipment (silencers, redesigned machinery)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">0.75 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Green belts around industrial complexes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">0.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s11q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s11q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is the iron and steel industry concentrated in and around the Chota Nagpur plateau region?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The Chota Nagpur Plateau region (covering Jharkhand, Odisha, Chhattisgarh, and West Bengal) has the maximum concentration of iron and steel industries in India due to the following relative locational advantages:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Low Cost of Iron Ore:</strong> High-grade hematite iron ore is available in abundance within the immediate vicinity (e.g., Singhbhum in Jharkhand, Mayurbhanj and Kendujhar in Odisha).</li>\n  <li><strong>High-Grade Raw Materials in Close Proximity:</strong> All required heavy smelting raw materials — coking coal (Jharia, Bokaro), limestone, dolomite (Sundargarh), and manganese — are found in close geographical proximity, drastically lowering assembly transport costs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cheap and Abundant Labour:</strong> Densely populated surrounding states of Bihar, West Bengal, Jharkhand, and Uttar Pradesh provide an abundant supply of cheap, skilled and unskilled labour.</li>\n  <li><strong>Vast Domestic and Export Market:</strong> Excellent rail and road connectivity links the region to the industrial heartlands of India and to the mega port of <strong>Kolkata</strong>, which provides export facilities and machinery import capabilities.</li>\n  <li><strong>Abundant Water and Hydroelectric Power:</strong> Perennial water supplies and cheap electricity are readily available from the Damodar Valley Corporation (DVC) and Subarnarekha river networks.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Proximity to high-grade iron ore mines (Singhbhum, Mayurbhanj)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Availability of coking coal, limestone, dolomite in close vicinity</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Low-cost skilled and unskilled workforce from neighbouring states</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Transport linkages and Kolkata port connectivity</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Water and hydroelectric supply from Damodar Valley Corporation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">0.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 11</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Case-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'In recent years, there is a tendency for sugar mills to shift and concentrate in the southern and western states, especially in Maharashtra. This is because the cane produced here has a higher sucrose content. The cooler climate also ensures a longer crushing season. Moreover, the cooperatives are more successful in these states.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> State three distinct geographic and organizational reasons why the sugar industry is migrating from northern India (UP/Bihar) to Maharashtra and South India.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Higher Sucrose Content:</strong> Tropical maritime climate yields thicker sugarcane with significantly higher sugar content per tonne compared to northern cane.</li><li><strong>Longer Crushing Season:</strong> The milder, cooler winter of peninsular India allows crushing operations to continue for several months longer without drying up cane juice.</li><li><strong>Flourishing Cooperative Sector:</strong> In Maharashtra, sugar mills are predominantly run by well-managed farmer cooperatives, ensuring smooth supply chains, stable payments, and modern automated machinery.</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> The Information Technology and Electronics industry has contributed significantly to employment generation in India, particularly for women.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> India has emerged as a premier global hub for software technology parks, BPOs, and hardware manufacturing, with Bengaluru known as the electronic capital of India.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> The IT sector employs millions of educated youth, and over 30 per cent of its workforce comprises women. Software Technology Parks across Bengaluru, Hyderabad, Pune, and Noida have generated vital foreign exchange earnings through IT exports.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(10)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(12)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "12": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch12\" data-book=\"geography\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">12</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Geography: Contemporary India – II</div>\n      <h2>Lifelines of National Economy</h2>\n      <p>Unit 12: Geography • Transport Networks, Ports, Pipelines, Communication & Tourism — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s12q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s12q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Answer the following in about 30 words: (i) State any three merits of roadways, (ii) Where and why is rail transport the most convenient means of transportation? (iii) What is the significance of the border roads? (iv) What is meant by trade? What is the difference between international and local trade?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>(i) State Any Three Merits of Roadways over Railways:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Lower Construction and Maintenance Cost:</strong> Construction cost of roads is much lower than that of railway lines.</li>\n  <li><strong>Traverses Difficult Terrains:</strong> Roads can traverse comparatively more dissected and undulating topography, can negotiate higher gradients of slopes, and can easily traverse mountains like the Himalayas where laying rail tracks is prohibitive.</li>\n  <li><strong>Door-to-Door Service:</strong> Road transport provides door-to-door service, which dramatically reduces the cost of loading and unloading goods. It acts as a vital feeder to railway stations, seaports, and airports.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>(ii) Where and Why is Rail Transport the Most Convenient Means of Transportation?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Region:</strong> The <strong>Northern Plains</strong> of India.</li>\n  <li><strong>Reasons:</strong> The vast level land, high population density, rich agricultural resources, and widespread industrial activity make the Northern Plains most favourable for laying rail tracks. Railways are the principal mode of freight and passenger transport for long distances.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(iii) Significance of Border Roads:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Constructed and maintained by the <strong>Border Roads Organisation (BRO)</strong> established in 1960.</li>\n  <li>They have improved accessibility in strategic border areas of the northern and north-eastern frontier (e.g., Ladakh, Arunachal Pradesh), facilitating swift armed forces movement for national defense and stimulating rapid economic development of remote border villages.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>(iv) What is Meant by Trade? Difference between International and Local Trade:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Trade:</strong> The exchange of goods and services among people, states, and countries through markets is referred to as trade.</li>\n  <li><strong>International Trade:</strong> Trade carried out between two or more sovereign countries across international borders. It involves foreign exchange currency and is an index of a nation's economic prosperity.</li>\n  <li><strong>Local Trade:</strong> Trade carried out within local jurisdictions — between cities, towns, and villages within the same country, using domestic currency.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Three distinct merits of roadways over railways</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Northern Plains suitability for railways (level land, dense population)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Significance of Border Roads Organisation (BRO) & defense logistics</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Trade definition and International vs Local trade distinction</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s12q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s12q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why are the means of transportation and communication called the lifelines of a nation and its economy?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Efficient means of transport and communication are rightly called the <strong>lifelines of a nation and its economy</strong> because of the following fundamental reasons:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Integration of Spatially Dispersed Markets:</strong> Production of goods takes place at specific locations, while consumers are scattered throughout the country. Transport bridges the distance between production centres and consumer markets, enabling seamless trade.</li>\n  <li><strong>Catalyst for Agricultural and Industrial Growth:</strong> Agriculture requires quick movement of fertilizers, seeds, and farm machinery, and transport of perishable produce (milk, vegetables) to urban centers. Industries depend on railways and road networks to carry bulky raw materials (iron ore, coal) and deliver manufactured goods across India and overseas.</li>\n  <li><strong>Socio-Cultural Unification:</strong> Transport networks and mass communication (radio, television, newspapers, Internet) knit people of diverse languages, cultures, and regions into a unified national community.</li>\n  <li><strong>National Defense and Disaster Management:</strong> In times of external military threats or natural disasters (floods, earthquakes, tsunamis), transport lifelines allow rapid deployment of armed forces, relief supplies, medicines, and rescue personnel to affected regions.</li>\n  <li><strong>Global Connectivity and Foreign Exchange:</strong> Airways, major seaports, and modern digital telecommunications connect India to the global economic grid, generating billions in international trade and tourism revenue.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Connecting production hubs to dispersed consumer markets</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Agricultural and industrial dependency on transport logistics</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Socio-cultural integration of diverse linguistic regions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Strategic military defense and emergency disaster relief</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Global trade, international ports and economic prosperity</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s12q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s12q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Write a note on the changing nature of international trade in India in the last fifteen years.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The nature of India’s international trade has undergone profound transformations over the last fifteen years:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Exchange of Commodities Replaced by Knowledge and Services:</strong> While exchange of traditional bulk commodities (iron ore, agricultural goods) continues, trade in information, software, and knowledge services has surged ahead. India has emerged as a global <strong>software superpower</strong>, earning massive foreign exchange through the export of information technology, software, and Business Process Outsourcing (BPO) services.</li>\n  <li><strong>Shifting Commodity Composition:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Major Exports:</em> Petroleum products (from domestic refining), gems and jewellery, engineering goods, chemicals, pharmaceuticals, and electronic goods have become dominant, replacing raw agricultural produce.</li>\n      <li><em>Major Imports:</em> Crude petroleum and petroleum products continue to be the largest import item, followed by electronic goods, machinery, gold, and precious stones.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Diversification of Trade Partners:</strong> India's trade is no longer dependent only on Britain or the US. It has expanded extensively with ASEAN countries, China, Japan, Middle East, Africa, and Latin America.</li>\n  <li><strong>Boom in Tourism as an Invisible Export:</strong> International tourism has grown by leaps and bounds. Foreign tourist arrivals contribute over Rs 1 lakh crore in foreign exchange annually. Tourism supports over 15 million people directly and promotes national integration, cultural heritage, and traditional handicrafts.</li>\n  <li><strong>Balance of Trade Dynamics:</strong> India maintains a trade deficit in merchandise due to heavy crude oil import bills, but compensates significantly through huge surpluses in service exports and foreign remittances.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Shift from commodity trade to IT/software service exports</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Changing export basket (gems, pharma, engineering, petroleum products)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Diversification of global trade partners beyond traditional West</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Role of tourism as a trade earning foreign exchange</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balance of trade dynamics (merchandise deficit vs service surplus)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">0.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 12</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Case-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'Pipelines are a new arrival on the transportation map of India. In the past, these were used to transport water to cities and industries. Now, these are used for transporting crude oil, petroleum products and natural gas from oil and natural gas fields to refineries, fertilizer factories and big thermal power plants. Gas from the Hazira-Vijaipur-Jagdishpur (HVJ) pipeline has fueled fertilizer plants in Uttar Pradesh.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> What are the initial and ongoing advantages and disadvantages of pipeline transport in India?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Merits:</strong> Initial cost of laying pipelines is high, but subsequent running costs are minimal. It rules out trans-shipment losses or transport delays caused by road congestion or rail strikes. Can be laid across difficult terrain and underwater.</li><li><strong>Demerit:</strong> Pipelines are inflexible (capacity cannot be expanded easily once laid), and underground leakages or ruptures are difficult to detect and repair immediately, posing environmental contamination risks.</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> Golden Quadrilateral Super Highways connect Delhi, Kolkata, Chennai, and Mumbai by six-lane super highways.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> The major objective of these Super Highway projects is to reduce time and distance between the mega cities of India, implemented by the National Highways Authority of India (NHAI).<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> The Golden Quadrilateral (along with North-South Corridor linking Srinagar to Kanyakumari and East-West Corridor linking Silchar to Porbandar) was constructed under the National Highways Development Project (NHDP) to speed up freight movement between economic powerhouses.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(11)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(13)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "13": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch13\" data-book=\"civics\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">13</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Democratic Politics – II (Civics)</div>\n      <h2>Power Sharing</h2>\n      <p>Unit 13: Civics • Belgian vs Sri Lankan Models, Prudential & Moral Reasons, Forms of Sharing — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s13q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s13q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the different forms of power sharing in modern democracies? Give an example of each of these.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>In modern democracies, power sharing arrangements take many different institutional forms:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Horizontal Distribution of Power (Among Organs of Government):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Power is shared among different organs of government, such as the <strong>Legislature</strong>, <strong>Executive</strong>, and <strong>Judiciary</strong>.</li>\n      <li>It is called horizontal because it allows different organs placed at the same level to exercise different powers. Such a separation ensures that none of the organs can exercise unlimited power. Each organ checks the others, resulting in a system of <strong>checks and balances</strong> (e.g., judges appointed by the executive can check the functioning of executive or laws made by the legislature).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Vertical Distribution of Power (Federal Division at Different Levels):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Power is shared among governments at different levels — a general government for the entire country (Central or Union Government) and governments at the provincial or sub-national level (State Governments).</li>\n      <li>In India, this is further extended downwards to local self-governments (Panchayats and Municipalities). It represents a vertical division from higher to lower tiers of governance.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Power Sharing among Different Social and Linguistic Groups:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Power may also be shared among different social groups, such as the religious and linguistic groups.</li>\n      <li><em>Example:</em> <strong>Community Government</strong> in Belgium, elected by people belonging to one language community (Dutch, French, or German-speaking) regardless of where they live, with power regarding cultural, educational, and language-related issues. In India, the system of 'reserved constituencies' in assemblies and parliament for SCs, STs, and women is another prime example.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Power Sharing among Political Parties, Pressure Groups and Movements:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>In a democracy, citizens must have freedom to choose among various contenders for power. Competition among different political parties ensures that power does not remain in one hand.</li>\n      <li>In contemporary politics, this takes the form of <strong>coalition governments</strong>, where two or more parties form an alliance to contest elections and share power if elected. Furthermore, interest groups (traders, industrialists, farmers, workers) share power through participation in governmental committees or bringing influence on decision-making.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Horizontal sharing & system of checks and balances</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Vertical federal division across central, state & local levels</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Social group power sharing (Belgian community govt, reserved seats)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Coalition governments, pressure groups & movement influence</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s13q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s13q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">State one prudential reason and one moral reason for power sharing with an example from the Indian context.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Both prudential and moral reasons support the vital need for power sharing in a democracy:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Prudential Reasons (Calculated Benefits and Risk Avoidance):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Concept:</em> Power sharing is desirable because it helps to reduce the possibility of conflict between social groups. Since social conflict often leads to violence and political instability, power sharing is a good way to ensure the stability of political order. Imposing the will of the majority community over others may look like an attractive option in the short run, but in the long run, it undermines the unity of the nation. Tyranny of the majority is not just oppressive for the minority; it often brings ruin to the majority as well.</li>\n      <li><em>Indian Example:</em> Reservation of seats in Lok Sabha and state assemblies for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes ensures that marginalized communities have a fair share in state power without resorting to violent alienation.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Moral Reasons (The Very Spirit of Democracy):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Concept:</em> Power sharing is the very spirit of democracy. A democratic rule involves sharing power with those affected by its exercise, and who have to live with its effects. People have a legitimate right to be consulted on how they are to be governed. A legitimate government is one where citizens, through participation, acquire a stake in the system. While prudential reasons stress that power sharing will bring out better outcomes, moral reasons emphasize the very act of power sharing as valuable.</li>\n      <li><em>Indian Example:</em> Decentralisation of governance through the 73rd and 74th Constitutional Amendments (1992), granting constitutionally protected power to rural Panchayats and urban Municipalities where common citizens participate directly in local decision-making.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Prudential reasons definition: Conflict avoidance & political stability</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Indian prudential example: Reserved constituencies for SC/ST</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Moral reasons definition: Intrinsic democratic spirit & citizen stake</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Indian moral example: Panchayati Raj & local self-government</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s13q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s13q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Compare the ways in which the Belgian and Sri Lankan governments solved the problem of cultural diversity.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison of Belgium and Sri Lanka in handling Cultural Diversity:</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;margin-top:8px;\">\n  <tr style=\"background:#f0fdfa;color:#115e59;\">\n    <th>Feature</th>\n    <th>Belgium (Accommodation Model)</th>\n    <th>Sri Lanka (Majoritarian Model)</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Ethnic Composition</strong></td>\n    <td>59% Dutch-speakers (Flemish region), 40% French-speakers (Wallonia), 1% German-speakers. In Brussels capital: 80% French, 20% Dutch.</td>\n    <td>74% Sinhala-speakers (mostly Buddhist), 18% Tamil-speakers (13% Sri Lankan Tamils, 5% Indian Tamils — Hindu and Muslim).</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Government Policy Approach</strong></td>\n    <td><strong>Accommodation:</strong> Between 1970 and 1993, the constitution was amended four times to work out an arrangement that would enable everyone to live together peacefully.</td>\n    <td><strong>Majoritarianism:</strong> The democratically elected government adopted a series of majoritarian measures to establish Sinhala supremacy (1956 Act).</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Key Institutional Measures</strong></td>\n    <td>1. Equal number of Dutch and French ministers in Central Government.<br>2. Many powers of central govt given to state governments.<br>3. Separate capital government for Brussels with equal representation.<br>4. Unique 'Community Government' for cultural/linguistic issues.</td>\n    <td>1. 1956 Act made Sinhala the sole official language, disregarding Tamil.<br>2. Preferential policies favored Sinhala applicants for university jobs.<br>3. State constitution mandated protecting and fostering Buddhism.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Ultimate Outcome</strong></td>\n    <td>Avoided civil strife, preserved national unity, and Brussels was chosen as the headquarters of the European Union.</td>\n    <td>Alienated Tamils, spawned LTTE militant struggle for Tamil Eelam, and plunged the country into a catastrophic decades-long civil war.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ethnic composition of Belgium and Sri Lanka contrasted</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Belgian accommodation approach & four constitutional amendments</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Sri Lankan majoritarian 1956 Act & Sinhala preferences</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Contrasting outcomes: Brussels EU headquarters vs Sri Lankan civil war</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 13</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Case-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'In 1956, an Act was passed in Sri Lanka to recognise Sinhala as the only official language, thus disregarding Tamil. The governments followed preferential policies that favoured Sinhala applicants for university positions and government jobs. A new constitution stipulated that the state shall foster Buddhism. All these government measures, coming one after the other, gradually increased the feeling of alienation among the Sri Lankan Tamils.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> What were the major grievances of Sri Lankan Tamils that ultimately led to the civil war?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Denial of Linguistic &amp; Cultural Rights:</strong> The 1956 Act made Sinhala the only official language, denying official status to Tamil and eroding their cultural identity.</li><li><strong>Systemic Discrimination in Jobs and Education:</strong> Preferential state policies systematically excluded Tamil youth from government employment and top university admissions.</li><li><strong>Religious Marginalisation:</strong> State patronage to Buddhism alienated Hindu, Muslim, and Christian Tamils, leading to demands for an independent state ('Tamil Eelam') and armed conflict.</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> Power sharing is good for democracies.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> It helps to reduce the possibility of conflict between social groups and ensures the political stability of the nation.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> Power sharing prevents the concentration of power in any single hand or social group. By accommodating diverse social interests, it averts mutual hostility and civil strife, forming the core prudential and moral justification of democratic governance.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(12)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(14)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "14": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch14\" data-book=\"civics\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">14</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Democratic Politics – II (Civics)</div>\n      <h2>Federalism</h2>\n      <p>Unit 14: Civics • Federal vs Unitary Systems, Decentralisation & Centre-State Relations — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s14q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s14q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the difference between a federal form of government and a unitary one? Explain with examples.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Distinction between a Federal Form of Government and a Unitary One:</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;margin-top:8px;\">\n  <tr style=\"background:#f0fdfa;color:#115e59;\">\n    <th>Feature</th>\n    <th>Federal Government</th>\n    <th>Unitary Government</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Levels of Government</strong></td>\n    <td>There are two or more tiers of government (e.g., Union Government and State Governments).</td>\n    <td>Either there is only one level of government or the sub-units are subordinate to the central government.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Constitutional Autonomy</strong></td>\n    <td>Different tiers govern the same citizens, but each tier has its own <strong>jurisdiction</strong> in specific matters of legislation, taxation, and administration guaranteed by the Constitution.</td>\n    <td>The central government can pass on orders to the provincial or the local government; sub-units possess no independent constitutional authority.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Constitutional Amendment</strong></td>\n    <td>Fundamental provisions of the constitution cannot be unilaterally changed by one level of government; requires consent of both levels.</td>\n    <td>The central legislature can amend the constitution unilaterally at will.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Role of Courts</strong></td>\n    <td>Courts have the power to interpret the constitution and the powers of different levels of government. The highest court acts as an umpire in disputes.</td>\n    <td>Judiciary does not adjudicate inter-governmental federal division of powers.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Examples</strong></td>\n    <td>India, USA, Canada, Australia, Switzerland, Belgium.</td>\n    <td>United Kingdom, France, China, Japan, Sri Lanka.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Levels of government comparison (Two or more tiers vs single tier)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Constitutional jurisdiction and autonomy comparison</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Rigidity of constitutional amendments in federations</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Role of Supreme Court as constitutional umpire and global examples</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s14q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s14q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">State the differences between 'coming together' federations and 'holding together' federations with examples.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Differences between 'Coming Together' Federations and 'Holding Together' Federations:</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;margin-top:8px;\">\n  <tr style=\"background:#f0fdfa;color:#115e59;\">\n    <th>Feature</th>\n    <th>'Coming Together' Federations</th>\n    <th>'Holding Together' Federations</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Formation Process</strong></td>\n    <td>Independent sovereign states come together on their own to form a bigger unit, pooling sovereignty and retaining identity to increase their security.</td>\n    <td>A large country decides to divide its power between the constituent states and the national government to accommodate regional diversity.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Power Distribution</strong></td>\n    <td>All the constituent states usually have equal power and are strong <em>vis-à-vis</em> the federal government.</td>\n    <td>The central government tends to be more powerful <em>vis-à-vis</em> the states. Often different constituent units have unequal powers (some granted special constitutional status).</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Examples</strong></td>\n    <td>USA, Switzerland, and Australia.</td>\n    <td>India, Spain, and Belgium.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Formation mechanism contrast (Independent states pooling vs large nation dividing)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Power parity between states and center contrasted</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Standard country examples for both categories</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s14q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s14q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How is federalism practised in India? Explain the three major policies.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Federalism is successfully practiced in India not merely because of constitutional provisions, but because of the nature of democratic politics in our country:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Creation of Linguistic States:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>In 1947, the boundaries of several old states of India were changed in order to create new states. This was done to ensure that people who spoke the same language lived in the same state (e.g., Andhra Pradesh, Maharashtra, Tamil Nadu).</li>\n      <li>Some states were created not on the basis of language but to recognize differences based on culture, ethnicity, or geography (e.g., Nagaland, Uttarakhand, and Jharkhand). Linguistic reorganisation made India more united and administration remarkably easier.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Language Policy:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Our Constitution did not give the status of national language to any one language. Hindi was identified as the official language, but Hindi is the mother tongue of only about 44 per cent of Indians. Therefore, there are <strong>22 languages recognized as Scheduled Languages</strong> by the Constitution.</li>\n      <li>States have their own official languages. The promotion of Hindi was accompanied by the continued use of English for official purposes, avoiding the linguistic conflict that plagued Sri Lanka.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Restructuring Centre-State Relations and Coalition Era:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>For a long time, the same party ruled both at the Centre and in most of the states. In those days, the Central Government would often misuse Article 356 to dismiss state governments controlled by rival parties.</li>\n      <li>All this changed significantly after <strong>1990</strong>, with the rise of regional political parties and the beginning of the era of <strong>coalition governments</strong> at the Centre. Since no single party got a clear majority in the Lok Sabha, major national parties entered into alliances with regional parties, leading to a new culture of power sharing and respect for the autonomy of State Governments, supported by landmark Supreme Court judgments.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Linguistic States policy and national integration impact</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.75 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Language policy (Hindi as official, 22 Scheduled Languages, English usage)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.75 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Centre-State relations transformation & post-1990 coalition era</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s14q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s14q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">State any four differences between the local government bodies before and after the Constitutional Amendment in 1992.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>A major step towards decentralisation was taken in 1992 through the <strong>73rd and 74th Constitutional Amendments</strong>. The status of local government changed fundamentally:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;margin-top:8px;\">\n  <tr style=\"background:#f0fdfa;color:#115e59;\">\n    <th>Feature</th>\n    <th>Local Government Before 1992</th>\n    <th>Local Government After 1992 (Constitutional Status)</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Conduct of Elections</strong></td>\n    <td>Elections were not held regularly; controlled at the arbitrary whim of the state government.</td>\n    <td>It is now <strong>constitutionally mandatory</strong> to hold regular elections to local government bodies every five years.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Election Management</strong></td>\n    <td>Managed under general state administration without an independent election authority.</td>\n    <td>An independent institution called the <strong>State Election Commission</strong> was created in each state to conduct panchayat and municipal elections.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Reservation for Marginalised</strong></td>\n    <td>No statutory reservation existed for SCs, STs, and OBCs.</td>\n    <td>Seats are reserved in the elected bodies and the executive heads of these institutions for <strong>Scheduled Castes, Scheduled Tribes, and Other Backward Classes</strong>.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Women’s Representation</strong></td>\n    <td>Negligible female representation in village decision-making.</td>\n    <td>At least <strong>one-third (33%)</strong> of all positions are reserved for women.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Revenue and Powers</strong></td>\n    <td>Local bodies had no powers or independent revenue resources of their own; completely dependent on state doles.</td>\n    <td>State governments are required to share some powers and revenue with local government bodies, supported by State Finance Commissions.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mandatory regular elections every 5 years vs irregular elections</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Creation of independent State Election Commission</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Statutory reservations for SC, ST, OBC and 1/3rd for women</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Constitutional sharing of powers and revenue with local bodies</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 14</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Case-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'The Constitution clearly provided a threefold distribution of legislative powers between the Union Government and the State Governments. Thus, it contains three lists: Union List, State List, and Concurrent List. Subjects that do not fall in any of the three lists are known as 'Residuary Subjects'.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> Name two subjects from each of the three lists and identify who makes laws on 'Residuary Subjects'.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Union List:</strong> Defense of the country, Foreign affairs, Banking, Communications, and Currency (Union Parliament alone can legislate).</li><li><strong>State List:</strong> Police, Trade, Commerce, Agriculture, and Irrigation (State Legislature alone can legislate).</li><li><strong>Concurrent List:</strong> Education, Forests, Trade Unions, Marriage, Adoption, and Succession (Both Centre and States can legislate; Centre's law prevails in case of conflict).</li><li><strong>Residuary Subjects:</strong> Computer software, e-commerce, and space research (According to the Constitution, the Union Government has the power to legislate on residuary subjects).</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> India is a 'holding together' federation.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> In India, the central government is constitutionally more powerful vis-à-vis the states, and some states like Jammu & Kashmir, Nagaland, and Mizoram have enjoyed special provisions under Article 371 due to historical and cultural reasons.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> Unlike 'coming together' federations (like the US) where constituent states have equal power, India decided to divide its powers between the national government and states to maintain unity amid immense linguistic and cultural diversity.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(13)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(15)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "15": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch15\" data-book=\"civics\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">15</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Democratic Politics – II (Civics)</div>\n      <h2>Gender, Religion and Caste</h2>\n      <p>Unit 15: Civics • Gender Division, Secularism, Communal Politics & Caste in Politics — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s15q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s15q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Mention different aspects of life in which women are discriminated against or disadvantaged in India.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>In India, women continue to face discrimination, disadvantage, and subjugation in several vital spheres of life:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Disparity in Literacy Rates:</strong> The literacy rate among women is only <strong>65.46%</strong> compared with <strong>82.14%</strong> among men (Census 2011). Similarly, a smaller proportion of girl students go for higher studies because parents prefer to spend their resources on boys’ education rather than spending equally on sons and daughters.</li>\n  <li><strong>Unpaid and Undervalued Work:</strong> On average, an Indian woman works one hour more than an average man every day, yet much of her work (cooking, cleaning, child rearing) is domestic and not paid, and therefore often not valued.</li>\n  <li><strong>Wage Disparity in Labour Markets:</strong> Under the <strong>Equal Remuneration Act, 1976</strong>, equal wages should be paid for equal work. However, in almost all spheres of work — from sports and cinema to factories and agricultural fields — women are paid less than men, even when both do exactly the same work.</li>\n  <li><strong>Declining Child Sex Ratio (Female Foeticide):</strong> In many parts of India, parents prefer to have sons and find ways to abort female fetuses before birth. This sex-selective abortion has led to a skewed child sex ratio in the country (falling as low as 850 in some districts).</li>\n  <li><strong>Violence and Exploitation:</strong> Women face widespread harassment, exploitation, and violence both inside the domestic home (domestic abuse, dowry harassment) and in public spaces, making urban areas increasingly unsafe.</li>\n  <li><strong>Extremely Low Political Representation:</strong> Women’s proportion in the Indian Lok Sabha has historically stayed below 15 per cent, and in state assemblies less than 10 per cent, far below the world average.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Literacy rate disparity (65% vs 82%) and higher education gap</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Unpaid domestic labour burden and lack of economic valuation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Violation of Equal Remuneration Act in labour wages</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Female foeticide and declining child sex ratio</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Domestic violence and low representation in legislatures</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s15q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s15q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">State the different forms that communalism can take in politics with examples.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Communalism can take various forms in democratic and electoral politics:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Everyday Religious Prejudices:</strong> The most common expression of communalism is in everyday beliefs. These routinely involve religious prejudices, stereotypes of religious communities, and belief in the inherent superiority of one’s religion over other religions.</li>\n  <li><strong>Quest for Political Dominance:</strong> A communal mind often leads to a quest for political dominance of one's own religious community. For those belonging to majority community, this takes the form of <strong>majoritarian dominance</strong> (e.g., Sinhala majoritarianism in Sri Lanka). For those belonging to the minority community, it can take the form of a desire to form a separate political unit.</li>\n  <li><strong>Political Mobilisation on Religious Lines:</strong> Political mobilization on religious lines is another frequent form of communalism. This involves the use of sacred religious symbols, religious leaders, emotional appeals, and plain fear in order to bring the followers of one religion together in the political arena to polarize votes during elections.</li>\n  <li><strong>Communal Violence, Riots and Massacres:</strong> Sometimes communalism takes its ugliest form of communal violence, riots, and massacres. India and Pakistan suffered some of the worst communal riots at the time of the Partition. Post-independence India has also seen large-scale communal violence in various states.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Everyday religious prejudices and stereotypes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Quest for majoritarian political dominance</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Electoral mobilization using religious symbols and emotional appeal</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Communal violence, partition riots and massacres</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s15q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s15q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">State two reasons to say that caste alone cannot determine election results in India.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Caste alone cannot determine election results in India due to the following structural electoral realities:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>No Single Caste Majority in Any Constituency:</strong> No parliamentary constituency in the country has a clear majority of one single caste. So, every candidate and party needs to win the confidence of more than one caste and community to win an election.</li>\n  <li><strong>No Party Wins All Votes of a Caste:</strong> No party wins the votes of all the voters of a caste or community. When people say that a caste is a 'vote bank' of one party, it usually means that a large proportion of the voters from that caste vote for that party, but never all.</li>\n  <li><strong>Multiple Candidates from the Same Caste:</strong> Many political parties may put up candidates from the same dominant caste (if that caste is believed to dominate the constituency). Some voters have more than one candidate from their caste, while other voters have no candidates from their caste.</li>\n  <li><strong>Voters Judge Performance of Incumbent Governments:</strong> The ruling party and the sitting MP or MLA frequently lose elections in our country. This could not have happened if all caste groups were frozen in their political loyalties. Voters actively consider inflation, employment, infrastructure, and leadership competence alongside caste identities.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Absence of single-caste majority in any Indian constituency</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Fragmentation of caste votes across competing parties</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Presence of multiple candidates from the same caste</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Anti-incumbency and economic performance determining outcomes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s15q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s15q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the constitutional provisions that make India a secular state.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The makers of the Indian Constitution chose the model of a <strong>Secular State</strong> to combat communalism and protect religious diversity:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>No Official State Religion:</strong> There is no official religion for the Indian state. Unlike the status of Buddhism in Sri Lanka, Islam in Pakistan, or Christianity in England, our Constitution does not give a special status to any religion.</li>\n  <li><strong>Freedom to Practice and Propagate Religion:</strong> The Constitution provides to all individuals and communities the fundamental freedom to profess, practice, and propagate any religion, or not to follow any (Articles 25–28).</li>\n  <li><strong>Prohibition of Discrimination:</strong> The Constitution strictly prohibits discrimination on grounds of religion in matters of education, employment, and public access (Article 15).</li>\n  <li><strong>State Intervention for Social Justice:</strong> At the same time, the Constitution allows the state to intervene in the matters of religion in order to ensure equality within religious communities (e.g., it bans untouchability under Article 17 and reforms family laws to ensure gender equity).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">No official religion for Indian republic (neutrality)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Fundamental right to profess, practice, and propagate religion</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Prohibition of religious discrimination under Article 15</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Legitimate state intervention to abolish untouchability & ensure social justice</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 15</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Case-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'Caste can take various forms in politics: when parties choose candidates, they keep in mind the caste composition of the electorate and nominate candidates from different castes so as to muster necessary support to win elections. When governments are formed, political parties usually take care that representatives of different castes and tribes find a place in it.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> How does politics also influence caste system in India?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Widening Caste Identity:</strong> Each caste group tries to become bigger by incorporating within it neighboring sub-castes or related castes which were earlier excluded.</li><li><strong>Formation of Caste Coalitions:</strong> Various caste groups are required to enter into a coalition with other castes or communities and thus enter into a dialogue and negotiation.</li><li><strong>New Categorizations:</strong> New kinds of caste groups have come up in the political arena like 'backward' and 'forward' caste groups. Politics thus politicizes caste, making it fluid and competitive.</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> The Women's Reservation Act (Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam) provides 33% reservation for women in Lok Sabha and state assemblies.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> Feminists and women’s movements argued that unless women control political power, their problems and discrimination will not get adequate attention.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> India has had one of the lowest percentages of women lawmakers in the world (under 15%). The passage of the Women's Reservation Bill ensures statutory representation of women at policy-making levels, emulating the successful model of Panchayati Raj.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(14)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(16)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "16": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch16\" data-book=\"civics\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">16</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Democratic Politics – II (Civics)</div>\n      <h2>Political Parties</h2>\n      <p>Unit 16: Civics • Meaning, Components, Functions, National & Regional Parties, Reforms — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s16q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s16q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is a political party? What are its components and what functions do political parties perform in a democracy?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Meaning of a Political Party:</strong></p>\n<p>A political party is a group of people who come together to contest elections and hold power in the government. They agree on some policies and programmes for the society with a view to promote the collective good.</p>\n\n<p><strong>2. Three Basic Components of a Political Party:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Leaders:</strong> Who frame policies, address rallies, and take strategic decisions.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Active Members:</strong> Who work on committees, mobilize supporters, and conduct party campaigns.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Followers:</strong> Common voters and citizens who believe in the party’s ideology and vote for its candidates.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>3. Vital Functions Performed by Political Parties in a Democracy:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Contest Elections:</strong> In most democracies, elections are fought mainly among the candidates put up by political parties. Parties select their candidates in different ways (by top leaders in India, or by party members in the US).</li>\n  <li><strong>Put Forward Policies and Programmes:</strong> Parties put forward different policies and programmes, and the voters choose from them. A party reduces a vast multitude of public opinions into a few basic directions which it supports.</li>\n  <li><strong>Making Laws:</strong> Parties play a decisive role in making laws for a country. Formally, laws are debated and passed in the legislature, but since most members belong to a party, they go by the direction of the party leadership irrespective of personal opinions.</li>\n  <li><strong>Form and Run Governments:</strong> Parties recruit leaders, train them, and then make them ministers to run the government in the way the party wants.</li>\n  <li><strong>Role of Opposition:</strong> Those parties that lose in the elections play the role of opposition to the parties in power, by voicing different views and criticizing government for its failures or wrong policies.</li>\n  <li><strong>Shape Public Opinion:</strong> They raise and highlight issues. Parties have lakhs of members and activists spread all over the country, launching movements for the resolution of problems faced by people.</li>\n  <li><strong>Access to Government Machinery:</strong> For an ordinary citizen, it is easy to approach a local party leader than a government officer. Parties serve as an accessible link between citizens and the state.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition and 3 components (Leaders, Active Members, Followers)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Core functions: Contesting elections, policy making & forming govt</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.75 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Crucial democratic functions: Opposition, public opinion & citizen access</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.75 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s16q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s16q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the various challenges faced by political parties in modern democracies?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Political parties across the globe face four major challenges that hinder their democratic effectiveness:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Lack of Internal Democracy within Parties:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>All over the world, there is a tendency in political parties towards the concentration of power in one or few leaders at the top.</li>\n      <li>Parties do not keep membership registers, do not hold regular organisational meetings, and do not conduct internal elections. Ordinary members do not get sufficient information on what happens inside the party. Disagreeing with the leadership means dismissal from the party.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Dynastic Succession:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Since most political parties do not practice open and transparent procedures for their functioning, it is very difficult for an ordinary worker to rise to the top.</li>\n      <li>In many parties, the top positions are always controlled by members of one family. This is unfair to other members and bad for democracy, since people who do not have adequate experience or popular support come to occupy positions of power.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Growing Role of Money and Muscle Power:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Since parties are focused only on winning elections, they tend to use short-cuts to win. They nominate those candidates who have or can raise lots of money.</li>\n      <li>Rich people and companies who give funds to the parties tend to have influence on the policies and decisions of the party. In many cases, parties support criminals who can secure votes through sheer intimidation.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Failure to Offer a Meaningful Choice to Voters:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>In recent years, there has been a significant decline in the ideological differences among parties in most parts of the world.</li>\n      <li>Parties agree on fundamental economic policies and differ only on minor details of implementation. Moreover, the same set of leaders keep shifting from one party to another, leaving voters with no genuine ideological alternative.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Lack of internal democracy, absence of organizational elections</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Dynastic succession and family monopolies over party leadership</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Role of money and muscle power (criminal candidates, corporate funding)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ideological convergence and failure to offer meaningful choice</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s16q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s16q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Suggest some reforms to strengthen political parties so that they perform their functions well.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>To strengthen political parties and cleanse democratic governance, several reforms have been enacted and further suggested:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Measures Already Enacted:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Anti-Defection Law:</em> The Constitution was amended to prevent elected MLAs and MPs from changing parties to secure ministerial berths or cash rewards. The law states that if any elected member changes parties, they lose their legislative seat.</li>\n      <li><em>Mandatory Affidavit of Assets and Criminal Cases:</em> The Supreme Court made it mandatory for every candidate contesting elections to file an <strong>Affidavit</strong> giving details of their property and criminal cases pending against them, empowering voters with crucial information.</li>\n      <li><em>Mandatory Organisational Elections and Tax Returns:</em> The Election Commission passed an order making it necessary for political parties to hold their organisational elections and file their income tax returns.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Suggested Reforms Yet to be Enacted:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Regulation of Internal Affairs by Law:</em> A law should be made to regulate the internal affairs of political parties. It should be made compulsory for political parties to maintain a register of their members, follow their own constitution, have an independent authority to act as a judge in case of party disputes, and hold open elections to the highest posts.</li>\n      <li><em>Reservation for Women:</em> It should be made mandatory for political parties to give a minimum proportion of tickets (about one-third) to women candidates.</li>\n      <li><em>State Funding of Elections:</em> There should be state funding of elections. The government should give parties money to support their election expenses in kind (petrol, paper, telephone) or cash on the basis of votes secured in previous elections, eliminating dependence on corrupt corporate funding.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Anti-defection law provisions and prevention of horse-trading</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Supreme Court affidavit mandate for wealth and criminal records</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Election Commission requirements for internal audits/elections</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Proposed laws for internal party democracy regulation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">State funding of elections and mandatory tickets for women</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 16</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Case-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'A party that secures at least six per cent of the total votes in an election to the Legislative Assembly of a State and wins at least two seats is recognised as a State party. A party that secures at least six per cent of the total votes in Lok Sabha elections or Assembly elections in four States and wins at least four seats in the Lok Sabha is recognised as a National party.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> State the Election Commission's criteria for recognizing a National Party and name two recognized National Parties in India.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>National Party Criteria:</strong> Must secure at least 6% of total valid votes in Lok Sabha elections OR in assembly elections across 4 states, AND win at least 4 seats in the Lok Sabha.</li><li><strong>Recognised National Parties:</strong> Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP), Indian National Congress (INC), Bahujan Samaj Party (BSP), Communist Party of India (Marxist) [CPI-M], and Aam Aadmi Party (AAP).</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> Democracies that have a multi-party system often appear very messy and lead to political instability.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> At the same time, this system allows a variety of interests and opinions to enjoy political representation.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> Multi-party systems (like India's) often require coalition arrangements that can seem volatile, but in socially and linguistically diverse countries, a multi-party system is essential to ensure that diverse regional, ethnic, and linguistic communities have an effective voice in national governance.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(15)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(17)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "17": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch17\" data-book=\"civics\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">17</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Democratic Politics – II (Civics)</div>\n      <h2>Outcomes of Democracy</h2>\n      <p>Unit 17: Civics • Accountable & Legitimate Government, Economic Growth, Dignity & Freedom — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s17q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s17q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does democracy produce an accountable, responsive and legitimate government?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Democracy produces an accountable, responsive, and legitimate government through the following institutional processes:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Accountable Government:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>In a democracy, people have the right to choose their rulers and people have control over the rulers. Whenever possible and necessary, citizens should be able to participate in decision making that affects them all.</li>\n      <li>Democracy ensures that decision making is based on norms and procedures. A citizen who wants to know if a decision was taken through the correct procedures can find this out through the <strong>Right to Information (RTI)</strong>. This transparency is completely missing in non-democratic regimes.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Responsive Government:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Democracies are expected to produce a government that is attentive to the needs and demands of the people and is largely free of corruption.</li>\n      <li>Public debates in media, civil society protests, judicial interventions, and fear of losing upcoming elections compel democratic rulers to listen to citizens' grievances and respond promptly.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Legitimate Government:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>A democratic government may be slow, less efficient, and not always very clean, but it is <strong>people’s own government</strong>.</li>\n      <li>It derives its sovereign authority directly from regular, free, and fair elections where citizens vote. There is an overwhelming support for the idea of democracy all over the world because people wish to be ruled by representatives elected by them. Democracy’s ability to generate its own support is itself an outcome that cannot be ignored.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Accountability through transparency, procedures & Right to Information</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.75 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Responsiveness to public opinion, protests & electoral accountability</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Legitimacy as 'people's own government' backed by free/fair elections</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.75 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s17q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s17q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the conditions under which democracies accommodate social diversities?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Democracies accommodate social diversities and prevent internal civil conflicts by fulfilling two essential conditions:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Condition 1: Democracy is not simply rule by majority opinion:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>The majority always needs to work with the minority so that governments function to represent the general view. Majority and minority opinions are not permanent; they shift with each issue and debate.</li>\n      <li>A genuine democracy listens to dissenting voices, minority concerns, and diverse perspectives rather than steamrolling through numerical supremacy.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Condition 2: Rule by majority must not become rule by majority community:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Rule by majority must not become rule by majority community in terms of religion, race, or linguistic group.</li>\n      <li>Rule by majority means that in case of every decision or in the election of every candidate, different persons and groups may and can form a majority. Democracy remains democracy only as long as every citizen has a chance of being in majority at some point in time.</li>\n      <li>If someone is barred from being in majority on the basis of birth, caste, or religion, then the democratic rule ceases to be accommodating for that person or group.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Institutional Mechanisms for Accommodation:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Democracies negotiate differences through constitutional power sharing, federal division of power, minority rights guarantees, and secular laws (as successfully shown by the Belgian model).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Condition 1: Majority working with minority, shifting opinions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Condition 2: Majority rule distinct from majority religious/ethnic community</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Institutional guarantees preventing permanent exclusion based on birth</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s17q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s17q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does democracy promote the dignity and freedom of citizens?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Democracy stands much superior to any other form of government in promoting the <strong>dignity and freedom of the individual</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Rooted in Respect and Freedom:</strong> The passion for respect and freedom are the basis of democracy. Throughout history, societies across the world were male-dominated, feudal, or based on caste oppression. Democracies recognize that all individuals are equal before the law and have an inherent right to live with dignity.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dignity of Women:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Long struggles by women have created some sensitivity today that respect to and equal treatment of women are necessary ingredients of a democratic society.</li>\n      <li>Once the democratic principle is recognized constitutionally, it becomes easier for women to wage a legal and moral struggle against patriarchal practices. In a non-democratic regime, this moral and legal backing would be absent because the principle of individual freedom is not accepted.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Dignity of Disadvantaged and Discriminated Castes:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Democracy in India has strengthened the claims of the disadvantaged and discriminated castes for equal status and equal opportunity.</li>\n      <li>Caste inequalities and untouchability have been legally abolished. While social discrimination still persists in practice, marginalized communities have the constitutional right to vote, organize protests, and contest elections, transforming them into active, vocal citizens.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Public Scrutiny and Citizen Empowerment:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>The most distinctive thing about democracy is that its examination never gets over. As democracy passes one test, it produces another test. As people get some benefits of democracy, they ask for more and want to make democracy even better.</li>\n      <li>The fact that people are complaining is itself a testimony to the success of democracy: it shows that people have transformed from subjects into active citizens who hold rulers accountable.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Inherent democratic principle of individual respect and freedom</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Legal and moral support for women's struggle against patriarchy</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Empowerment and equal status for historically marginalized castes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Citizen awareness and complaints as supreme testimony of democratic success</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 17</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Case-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'Evidence shows that in practice many democracies did not fulfill the expectation of producing economic growth and eliminating poverty. Between 1950 and 2000, dictatorships have had slightly higher rates of economic growth. But when we compare economic growth rates in poor countries under dictatorship and democracy, the difference is negligible. Economic development depends on several factors: country’s population size, global situation, cooperation from other countries, economic priorities adopted by the country, etc.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> Should democracy be rejected because dictatorships have shown slightly higher economic growth rates in some decades?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Negligible Economic Difference:</strong> When poor countries are compared, the difference in economic growth between democracy and dictatorship is virtually negligible. Economic growth depends on human capital, savings rate, and foreign trade policies, not autocratic rule.</li><li><strong>Overwhelming Positive Non-Economic Outcomes:</strong> Dictatorships deny basic human rights, free speech, and dignity. Democracy guarantees civil liberties, political equality, peaceful transfer of power, and rule of law, making democracy far superior overall.</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> A public expression of dissatisfaction with democracy shows the success of the democratic project.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> It transforms people from the status of a 'subject' into that of a 'citizen' who believe that their vote makes a difference to how the government is run.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> In an autocracy, public dissatisfaction is met with imprisonment and censorship. In a democracy, citizens' complaints reflect critical political consciousness and confidence that their democratic agency can reform government policies.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(16)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(18)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "18": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch18\" data-book=\"economics\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">18</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Economics: Understanding Economic Development</div>\n      <h2>Development</h2>\n      <p>Unit 18: Economics • National Income, Per Capita Criteria, HDI & Sustainability — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s18q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s18q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why do different persons have different notions of development? Which of the following explanations is more important and why? Explain with examples how development for one may be destructive for another.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Different Persons can have Different Development Goals:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Different persons have different notions of development because people's life situations are different. People seek things that are most important for them, that which can fulfill their specific aspirations or desires.</li>\n  <li><em>Examples:</em>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Landless Rural Labourer:</strong> More days of work and better wages; quality education for children in local government schools; no social discrimination.</li>\n      <li><strong>Prosperous Farmer in Punjab:</strong> High family income through higher support prices for crops, hardworking and cheap migrant labour, and ability to settle children abroad.</li>\n      <li><strong>An Adivasi in Narmada Valley:</strong> Protection of ancestral forest lands, no displacement by large dams, and preservation of community culture.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>2. What may be Development for One may be Destructive for Another:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>At times, two persons or groups may seek things which are conflicting. A girl expects as much freedom and opportunity as her brother, and that he also shares in the household work; her brother may not like this.</li>\n  <li>Similarly, industrialists may want more dams to get more electricity, but constructing large dams submerges the land and disrupts the lives of indigenous people and farmers whose lands are inundated. Hence, industrial development for one causes ecological destruction and displacement for another.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Concept: Different people have different development goals</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Contrasting examples (landless labourer vs prosperous farmer)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Conflicting development goals: Industrial dam vs tribal displacement</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s18q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s18q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the non-material goals that people seek besides a higher income?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Besides income, people seek other non-material goals that are equally or more important for a good quality of life:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Equal Treatment and Respect:</strong> People desire to be treated equally without discrimination based on caste, religion, or gender. Respect from others is fundamental to self-esteem and human dignity.</li>\n  <li><strong>Freedom and Liberty:</strong> Freedom to think, express oneself, practice one's faith, and make career and life choices without fear of persecution or arbitrary state harassment.</li>\n  <li><strong>Security of Life and Livelihood:</strong> A secure job that offers guaranteed income and safety in the workplace is often preferred over a higher-paying job with no job security and high physical danger.</li>\n  <li><strong>Clean and Healthy Environment:</strong> A pollution-free physical environment, access to unadulterated food, clean drinking water, and quality healthcare are vital determinants of well-being that money alone cannot buy.</li>\n  <li><strong>Social Harmony and Peace:</strong> Living in a peaceful community free from social conflict, crime, and violence allows families to thrive. Money cannot protect individuals from infectious epidemics or communal violence unless the whole community takes preventive steps.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Equal treatment and freedom as non-material goals</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Job security and safe working environment</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Clean, pollution-free living environment and health</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Social harmony, peace, and collective well-being</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s18q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s18q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the main criterion used by the World Bank in classifying different countries? What are the limitations of this criterion? How does the UNDP criterion differ from it?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison of World Bank and UNDP Criteria for Measuring Development:</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;margin-top:8px;\">\n  <tr style=\"background:#f0fdfa;color:#115e59;\">\n    <th>Feature</th>\n    <th>World Bank Criteria (World Development Report)</th>\n    <th>UNDP Criteria (Human Development Report)</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Primary Indicator</strong></td>\n    <td>Relies exclusively on a single economic indicator: <strong>Per Capita Income (Average Income)</strong>.</td>\n    <td>Uses a comprehensive multi-dimensional index: <strong>Human Development Index (HDI)</strong>.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Dimensions Covered</strong></td>\n    <td>Only monetary income (measured in US Dollars using Purchasing Power Parity).</td>\n    <td>Combines three vital dimensions:\n      <ol>\n        <li><strong>Health:</strong> Life Expectancy at birth.</li>\n        <li><strong>Education:</strong> Mean and Expected years of schooling.</li>\n        <li><strong>Income:</strong> Per Capita Gross National Income (GNI).</li>\n      </ol>\n    </td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Country Classification</strong></td>\n    <td>Divides countries into High Income (rich countries), Middle Income, and Low Income.</td>\n    <td>Ranks all nations on a global index (0 to 1), categorizing them into Very High, High, Medium, and Low Human Development.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Limitations / Merits</strong></td>\n    <td><em>Limitation:</em> It hides glaring economic inequalities. It tells us nothing about how income is distributed among citizens.</td>\n    <td><em>Merit:</em> It provides a holistic assessment of human capabilities and citizens' real living standards.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">World Bank's Per Capita Income criteria explained</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">UNDP's Human Development Index (HDI) three dimensions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Comparative critique: Income averages vs health & education realities</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s18q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s18q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is the issue of sustainability important for development? Explain with examples of groundwater overuse and crude oil depletion.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The issue of sustainability of development is crucial for humankind because development that depletes the natural resource base today cannot be sustained for future generations:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Overuse of Groundwater in India:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Recent evidence suggests that groundwater is under serious threat of overuse in many parts of the country. About <strong>300 districts</strong> have reported a water level decline of over 4 metres during the past 20 years.</li>\n      <li>Nearly one-third of the country is over-pumping its groundwater reserves. In agricultural heartlands like Punjab, Haryana, and western UP, excessive tube-well pumping for cash crops has severely depleted aquifers. If current trends continue, 60 per cent of the country will be doing the same in the next 25 years, causing an unprecedented drinking water and agricultural crisis.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Exhaustion of Global Crude Oil Reserves:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Crude oil is a non-renewable natural resource. According to global estimates, present reserves of crude oil will last only around 40 to 50 years if extracted at the current rate.</li>\n      <li>Countries like India, which depend on imports for over 85% of their crude oil, face immense economic vulnerability due to rising oil prices and geopolitical volatility.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Inter-Generational Equity:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Sustainable development embodies the principle that <em>'We have not inherited the Earth from our ancestors; we have borrowed it from our children.'</em></li>\n      <li>Development must focus on renewable energy (solar, wind), eco-friendly technologies, and recycling so that present progress does not compromise the survival of future generations.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Groundwater depletion crisis in India (300 districts, 4-metre decline)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.75 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Exhaustion of non-renewable fossil fuels (crude oil 50-year limit)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Inter-generational equity and sustainable renewable solutions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.75 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 18</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Data-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Examine the following comparative data and answer the question:</strong><br><table border='1' cellpadding='6' style='border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:6px;'><tr><th>State</th><th>Per Capita Income (₹)</th><th>Infant Mortality Rate (per 1000)</th><th>Literacy Rate (%)</th></tr><tr><td>Haryana</td><td>2,36,147</td><td>30</td><td>82</td></tr><tr><td>Kerala</td><td>2,04,105</td><td>10</td><td>94</td></tr><tr><td>Bihar</td><td>40,982</td><td>35</td><td>62</td></tr></table><br><strong>Question:</strong> Why does Kerala have a much lower Infant Mortality Rate and higher Literacy Rate than Haryana despite having a lower per capita income?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adequate Provision of Basic Public Facilities:</strong> Kerala has a lower infant mortality rate (10 per 1,000) because it has adequate provision of basic health and educational facilities established over decades through sustained public investment.</li><li><strong>Well-Functioning Public Distribution System (PDS):</strong> Kerala maintains an efficient PDS network that ensures nutritional security and affordable food grains for poor households, proving that money alone cannot buy all health and educational security.</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> Per capita income does not reveal how income is distributed among the citizens of a country.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> Two countries with identical per capita income can have radically different social realities — one country may have equitable distribution where most citizens are moderately prosperous, while another may have a handful of multi-billionaires and the vast majority living in extreme poverty.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> Average income is useful for broad international comparisons, but it completely obscures internal disparities. Hence, development must be assessed through multidimensional criteria like education, health, and equitable distribution, as done in the UNDP's Human Development Index.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(17)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(19)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "19": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch19\" data-book=\"economics\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">19</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Economics: Understanding Economic Development</div>\n      <h2>Sectors of the Indian Economy</h2>\n      <p>Unit 19: Economics • Primary, Secondary & Tertiary, Disguised Unemployment, Organised vs Unorganised — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s19q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s19q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the difference between primary, secondary and tertiary sectors using examples.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Primary Sector:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Activities undertaken by directly using natural resources. It forms the base for all other products that we subsequently make. Since most of the natural products we get are from agriculture, dairy, fishing, and forestry, this sector is also called the <strong>agriculture and related sector</strong>.</li>\n  <li><em>Examples:</em> Cultivation of cotton, dairy farming, extraction of mineral ores, fishing.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>2. Secondary Sector:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Covers activities in which natural products are changed into other forms through ways of manufacturing that we associate with industrial activity. The product is not produced by nature but has to be made. It is also known as the <strong>industrial sector</strong>.</li>\n  <li><em>Examples:</em> Spinning yarn and weaving cloth from cotton fiber, making sugar or gur from sugarcane, manufacturing bricks from earth, producing steel from iron ore.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>3. Tertiary Sector:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>These activities help in the development of the primary and secondary sectors. These activities, by themselves, do not produce a good, but they are an aid or support for the production process. Since these activities generate services rather than physical goods, the tertiary sector is also called the <strong>service sector</strong>.</li>\n  <li><em>Examples:</em> Transport (trucks, trains), storage, banking, insurance, trade, telecommunications, healthcare, and education.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Primary sector definition and natural resource extraction examples</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.75 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Secondary sector manufacturing transformation and examples</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.75 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Tertiary sector service/support functions and examples</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s19q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s19q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is the tertiary sector becoming so important in India? Give four reasons.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Over the last forty years, the tertiary (service) sector has emerged as the largest producing sector in India, replacing the primary sector, due to four major reasons:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Demand for Basic Public Services:</strong> In any country, several services such as hospitals, educational institutions, post and telegraph services, police stations, courts, defense, transport, banks, and municipal administration are required. These are regarded as <strong>basic services</strong>. In a developing country, the government has to take responsibility for the provision of these services.</li>\n  <li><strong>Development of Agriculture and Industry:</strong> The development of agriculture and industry directly stimulates the demand for services such as transport, trade, storage, marketing, and commercial banking. Greater the agricultural and industrial production, greater the demand for such support services.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rising Income Levels and Consumer Spending:</strong> As income levels rise, sections of people start demanding many more services like dining out in restaurants, tourism, shopping, private hospitals, private schools, and professional training centers, especially in cities.</li>\n  <li><strong>Information and Communication Technology (ICT) Revolution:</strong> Over the past decade, certain new services such as those based on information and communication technology, software development, BPOs, and digital data handling have become essential and grown rapidly.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Provision of essential basic services (health, education, police)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Growth of agriculture and industry demanding transport/trade</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Rising consumer disposable incomes creating lifestyle service demand</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Information and communication technology revolution and exports</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s19q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s19q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is disguised unemployment? Explain with an example each from rural and urban areas.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Meaning of Disguised Unemployment:</strong></p>\n<p>Disguised unemployment (also called <strong>underemployment</strong>) is a situation where more people are engaged in an activity than are actually required. Each person appears to be working, but their potential is not fully utilised. If some people are removed, total production does not fall at all; their marginal productivity is zero.</p>\n\n<p><strong>2. Example from Rural Areas:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Take the case of a small farmer, Laxmi, owning a two-hectare plot of unirrigated land. All five members of her family work on the plot throughout the year. They have nowhere else to go for work.</li>\n  <li>Each one is doing some work, but no one is fully employed. If two members of the family move out to work on a nearby construction project or a landlord's field to earn extra wages, the output from Laxmi's farm does not decline. The three remaining members can cultivate the plot just as effectively. The two extra members were <strong>disguisedly unemployed</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>3. Example from Urban Areas:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>In urban areas, disguised unemployment is prevalent in the informal service sector. Thousands of casual workers on the streets — painters, plumbers, repair persons, and cart-pullers — spend the whole day on the road, but earn very little.</li>\n  <li>Similarly, in small family-run retail grocery shops, four family members sit in the shop where only two are needed to serve customers. They work because they lack alternative gainful employment opportunities.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition: Underemployment where marginal productivity is zero</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Rural agricultural example (Laxmi's family on small plot)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.75 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Urban informal service sector example (casual street workers, family shops)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.75 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s19q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s19q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Compare the employment conditions prevailing in the organised and unorganised sectors.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison of Employment Conditions in Organised and Unorganised Sectors:</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;margin-top:8px;\">\n  <tr style=\"background:#f0fdfa;color:#115e59;\">\n    <th>Feature</th>\n    <th>Organised Sector</th>\n    <th>Unorganised Sector</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Registration &amp; Laws</strong></td>\n    <td>Registered by the government and has to follow its rules and regulations formulated under various laws (Factories Act, Minimum Wages Act, Payment of Gratuity Act).</td>\n    <td>Small and scattered units outside the control of the government. Rules and regulations exist on paper, but are largely flouted.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Security of Employment</strong></td>\n    <td>Workers have <strong>job security</strong>; they cannot be terminated without proper formal procedure and notice.</td>\n    <td>Employment is not secure. People can be asked to leave at any time without reason, especially during slack seasons.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Working Hours &amp; Overtime</strong></td>\n    <td>Fixed working hours. If workers work more, they have to be paid overtime wages by the employer.</td>\n    <td>Working hours are often long (12–14 hours) with no provision for overtime payment.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Benefits &amp; Leave</strong></td>\n    <td>Paid leave, payment during holidays, provident fund (PF), gratuity, and medical benefits guaranteed.</td>\n    <td>No paid leave, no holiday wages, no medical care, and no pension or social security benefits.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>Working Conditions</strong></td>\n    <td>Safe working environment with clean drinking water and sanitary conditions.</td>\n    <td>Often dangerous, unhygienic, and hazardous working conditions.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Registration under labor laws vs evasion of regulations</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Job security contrast (formal tenure vs hire-and-fire)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Fixed hours and overtime compensation contrast</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Social security (PF, paid leave, gratuity, medical benefits)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Safe physical working environment comparison</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 19</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Case-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'Under MGNREGA 2005 (Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act), all those who are able to, and are in need of, work in rural areas are guaranteed 100 days of employment in a year by the government. If the government fails in its duty to provide employment, it will give unemployment allowances to the people.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> How does MGNREGA 2005 help in creating durable rural assets while providing economic security?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Statutory Right to Work:</strong> Guarantees 100 days of wage employment per rural household, preventing distress rural-to-urban migration during agricultural lean seasons.</li><li><strong>Durable Asset Creation:</strong> The work projects undertaken (pond deepening, canal excavation, check-dam construction, tree planting) directly conserve soil and water, drought-proofing the village and boosting agricultural productivity in subsequent seasons.</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> In India, the primary sector continues to be the largest employer, even though its share in GDP has shrunk drastically.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> Secondary and tertiary sectors failed to create enough jobs to absorb the surplus agricultural labour force.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> While industrial output grew significantly, secondary and tertiary sector employment did not expand at the same pace. Consequently, nearly 44% of Indian workers remain stuck in agriculture producing less than 18% of GDP, resulting in severe disguised unemployment.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(18)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(20)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "20": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch20\" data-book=\"economics\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">20</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Economics: Understanding Economic Development</div>\n      <h2>Money and Credit</h2>\n      <p>Unit 20: Economics • Medium of Exchange, Formal vs Informal Credit, Terms of Credit & SHGs — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s20q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s20q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does money solve the problem of double coincidence of wants? Explain with an example of your own.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Meaning of Double Coincidence of Wants:</strong></p>\n<p>In a barter system where goods are directly exchanged without the use of money, double coincidence of wants is an essential condition: what a person desires to sell must be exactly what the other person wishes to buy. Both parties have to agree to sell and buy each other’s commodities.</p>\n\n<p><strong>2. How Money Solves the Problem:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Money eliminates the cumbersome need for double coincidence of wants by acting as an intermediate in the exchange process, known as a <strong>medium of exchange</strong>.</li>\n  <li><em>Original Example:</em> Suppose a shoe manufacturer wants to sell shoes in the market and buy wheat. Under a barter system, the shoe manufacturer would have to find a wheat-growing farmer who not only wants to sell wheat, but also simultaneously wants to buy shoes. Finding such an exact match is extremely difficult.</li>\n  <li>With money, the shoe manufacturer simply sells shoes for rupees in the open market and then uses that money to purchase wheat from any farmer or grocer selling wheat. The transaction is split into two independent, effortless steps.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Barter system double coincidence of wants explained</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Money as an intermediate medium of exchange</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Practical example illustrating elimination of exchange frictions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s20q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s20q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">In situations with high risks, credit might create further problems for the borrower. Explain with the help of an example.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>In high-risk situations (particularly small-scale crop cultivation), credit can push the borrower into a devastating <strong>debt trap</strong> instead of improving their earnings:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Case Analysis of a Small Farmer (Swapna):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Swapna, a small farmer, borrows money from a local moneylender at high interest rates to meet the expenses of cultivating groundnuts on her 3 acres of land, hoping that her harvest would repay the loan.</li>\n      <li>Midway through the season, pests attack the crop and the crop fails despite spraying expensive pesticides. She cannot repay the moneylender, and the debt grows over the year into a large amount.</li>\n      <li>Next year, she takes a fresh loan for cultivation. It is a normal crop this year, but the earnings are not enough to cover the old debt plus the new loan.</li>\n      <li>She is caught in a <strong>debt trap</strong>. To clear the loan, she is forced to sell off a portion of her valuable ancestral farmland.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Structural Vulnerability:</strong> In crop production, the risk of failure is high due to unpredictable monsoon rains, pest attacks, or price crashes in the mandi. If crops fail, credit turns from an instrument of growth into an instrument of financial ruin, leaving the borrower in a far worse situation than before.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Concept of debt trap where credit pushes borrower into ruin</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Swapna case study: Crop failure, compounding debt, land sale</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Agricultural risk factors (monsoon, pests) exacerbating informal credit</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s20q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s20q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">In what ways does the Reserve Bank of India supervise the functioning of banks? Why is this necessary?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) plays a crucial supervisory role over the formal banking sector in India:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Maintenance of Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR):</strong> The RBI monitors that commercial banks actually keep a minimum cash balance out of the deposits they receive. In India, banks currently maintain about 4.5% to 15% of their deposits as cash reserve to cater to daily withdrawal demands of depositors.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ensuring Credit Delivery to Priority Sectors:</strong> The RBI sees that banks give loans not just to profit-making businesses, large traders, and corporate houses, but also to small cultivators, small-scale industries, small borrowers, and SHGs. This is enforced through <strong>Priority Sector Lending (PSL)</strong> norms.</li>\n  <li><strong>Monitoring Interest Rates and Terms:</strong> Periodically, banks have to submit information to the RBI on how much they are lending, to whom, and at what interest rates, ensuring that commercial banks do not exploit vulnerable citizens with usurious terms.</li>\n  <li><strong>Why Supervision is Indispensable:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Without RBI supervision, commercial banks would operate solely for private profit maximization, lending exclusively to rich corporate entities in cities and neglecting poor rural farmers.</li>\n      <li>It prevents bank failures, safeguards public deposits, maintains confidence in the financial system, and promotes balanced regional development.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Monitoring minimum Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Enforcing priority sector lending to small farmers/borrowers</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Periodic reporting of lending rates and portfolio scrutiny</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Why supervision is vital: Financial stability & preventing corporate bias</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s20q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s20q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the basic idea behind the Self-Help Groups (SHGs) for the poor? Explain in your own words.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Self-Help Groups (SHGs)</strong> have emerged as an extraordinary institutional innovation to empower poor women and overcome the barriers of formal bank credit:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Basic Structure:</strong> A typical SHG consists of 15 to 20 members, usually belonging to one neighbourhood, who meet and save money regularly. Saving per member varies from ₹25 to ₹100 or more, depending on the ability to save.</li>\n  <li><strong>Small Internal Loans without Collateral:</strong> Members can take small loans from the group itself to meet their immediate emergency needs (buying seeds, fertilizers, school fees, releasing mortgaged land). The group charges interest on these loans, but this is far lower than what informal moneylenders charge.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bank Linkage and Collective Guarantee:</strong> After a year or two, if the group is regular in savings, it becomes eligible for availing bank loans. The loan is sanctioned in the name of the group to create self-employment opportunities (buying sewing machines, cattle, handlooms). The formal bank lends to poor women <strong>without individual collateral</strong> because the entire SHG provides collective guarantee; any non-repayment is followed up rigorously by other members.</li>\n  <li><strong>Financial Independence for Women:</strong> It frees poor rural women from the clutches of predatory moneylenders, enabling them to build assets and generate steady livelihoods.</li>\n  <li><strong>Platform for Social Empowerment:</strong> The regular meetings of the SHG provide an active platform to discuss and act on a variety of social issues like health, nutrition, child marriage, and domestic violence.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Structure and regular savings mechanism of SHGs (15-20 members)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Internal low-interest emergency loans</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Overcoming lack of collateral through collective bank linkage</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Economic self-reliance and asset building for poor women</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Social empowerment platform discussing health and domestic issues</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 20</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Case-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'Every loan agreement specifies an interest rate which the borrower must pay to the lender along with the repayment of the principal. In addition, lenders may demand collateral (security) against loans. Collateral is an asset that the borrower owns (such as land, building, vehicle, livestock, deposits with banks) and uses this as a guarantee to a lender until the loan is repaid.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> Why do formal commercial banks insist on collateral before granting loans, and how does this requirement exclude poor borrowers?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Risk Mitigation for Banks:</strong> Collateral serves as a legal guarantee; if the borrower defaults, the lender has the right to sell the asset to recover loan dues.</li><li><strong>Exclusion of the Poor:</strong> Poor rural labourers and small artisans lack land deeds, permanent houses, or bank deposits to pledge as collateral. Consequently, formal banks refuse them loans, forcing them to borrow from exploitative local moneylenders at exorbitant interest rates (36%–60% per annum).</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> Cheap and affordable credit is crucial for a country's economic development.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> High interest rates charged by informal lenders mean that a large part of the earnings of the borrowers is spent to repay the loan, leaving them with little income for reinvestment and growth.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> When farmers and small entrepreneurs have access to low-interest formal loans, they can invest in seeds, machinery, and business expansion, generating profits, higher employment, and accelerated national economic development.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(19)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(21)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "21": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch21\" data-book=\"economics\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">21</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Economics: Understanding Economic Development</div>\n      <h2>Globalisation and the Indian Economy</h2>\n      <p>Unit 21: Economics • MNCs, Trade Liberalisation, WTO & Fair Globalisation — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s21q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s21q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What do you understand by globalisation? Explain the various ways in which MNCs set up, or control, production in other countries.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Meaning of Globalisation:</strong></p>\n<p>Globalisation is the process of rapid integration or interconnection between countries. It involves the free flow of goods, services, investments, technology, and people across national boundaries, transforming the world into a single interconnected global market.</p>\n\n<p><strong>2. Major Ways MNCs Set Up or Control Production Across Countries:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Setting Up Production Jointly with Local Companies (Joint Ventures):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>MNCs enter into partnership agreements with existing domestic companies. This provides dual benefits to local firms: MNCs bring in additional capital for modern machines and introduce advanced production technology (e.g., Ford Motors entered India in partnership with Mahindra &amp; Mahindra).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Buying Up Local Companies and Expanding Production:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>The most common route for MNC investment is to buy up existing local companies and then expand production. MNCs with huge wealth can easily do so.</li>\n      <li><em>Example:</em> <strong>Cargill Foods</strong>, a very large American MNC, bought over smaller Indian companies such as <em>Parakh Foods</em>, gaining control over large marketing networks and four oil refineries to become the largest producer of edible oil in India.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Placing Orders with Small Producers (Contract Manufacturing):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Large MNCs in garments, footwear, and sports equipment place production orders with thousands of small producers around the world (including India, Bangladesh, and Vietnam).</li>\n      <li>The products are supplied to the MNCs, which then sell these under their own brand names (e.g., Nike, Zara, Adidas) to consumers, exerting tremendous power to determine price, quality, delivery, and labour conditions for distant producers.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition of globalisation as international integration</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Joint ventures with domestic companies (technology & capital)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Acquisition of domestic firms (Cargill Foods buying Parakh Foods)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Placing production contracts with small producers (brand selling)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s21q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s21q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What were the reasons for putting barriers to foreign trade and foreign investment by the Indian government after independence? Why did it wish to remove these barriers around 1991?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The trade policy of the Indian government underwent a historic reversal from protectionism (1947–1990) to liberalisation (post-1991):</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Reasons for Putting Barriers After Independence (1947):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>The Indian government, after independence, had put barriers to foreign trade and foreign investment to protect domestic infant industries from foreign competition.</li>\n      <li>Industries were just coming up in the 1950s and 1960s, and competition from imports at that nascent stage would not have allowed these domestic industries to survive. India allowed imports of only essential items such as machinery, fertilisers, and petroleum. All developed countries had used trade barriers during early stages of their industrialisation.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Reasons for Removing Barriers in 1991 (New Economic Policy):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Around 1991, severe balance-of-payments crisis and foreign exchange shortages forced a re-evaluation of policy.</li>\n      <li>The government felt that the time had come for Indian producers to compete with producers around the globe. It was felt that competition would improve the performance and quality of goods produced by domestic manufacturers.</li>\n      <li>Removing barriers (trade liberalisation) allowed businesses to freely import raw materials and modern machinery, while attracting foreign direct investment (FDI) from multinational corporations.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Post-independence protectionist barriers: Shielding infant industries</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">1991 liberalisation shift: Promoting global competition & efficiency</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s21q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s21q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">'The impact of globalisation has not been uniform.' Explain this statement with examples of both beneficiaries and sufferers.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The impact of globalisation in India has been deeply uneven, creating major beneficiaries alongside vulnerable groups that have suffered severely:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>The Big Winners (Advantaged Groups):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Urban Affluent Consumers:</em> Consumers have enjoyed greater choice, improved quality, and lower prices for products like automobiles, mobile phones, television, soft drinks, and packaged fast foods.</li>\n      <li><em>Top Indian Companies as MNCs:</em> Globalisation enabled top Indian companies to upgrade technology and emerge as multinationals themselves (e.g., Tata Motors in automobiles, Infosys in IT, Ranbaxy in pharma, Asian Paints, Sundaram Fasteners).</li>\n      <li><em>IT and Service Industry Professionals:</em> High-paying employment opportunities in software, data processing, call centers, and engineering consultancy.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>The Sufferers (Disadvantaged Groups):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Small Scale Manufacturers:</em> Global competition hit small producers hard in industries like batteries, capacitors, plastics, toys, tyres, dairy products, and vegetable oil. Many small units were forced to shut down because they could not compete with cheap, mass-produced Chinese imports, rendering millions of workers jobless.</li>\n      <li><em>Unorganised Industrial Labour:</em> To cut production costs in the face of fierce global competition, employers prefer to hire workers 'flexibly' on temporary contracts. Workers are denied permanent job contracts, health insurance, and paid leave, working 12–14 hour shifts with precarious incomes.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Benefits to urban consumers (wider choices, lower prices)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Emergence of Indian MNCs (Tata, Infosys, Ranbaxy)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Devastation of small-scale producers by cheap imports</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Erosion of labour security and rise of casual contracts</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 21</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Case-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'World Trade Organisation (WTO) is one such organisation whose aim is to liberalise international trade. Started at the initiative of the developed countries, WTO establishes rules regarding international trade, and sees that these rules are obeyed. About 164 countries of the world are currently members of the WTO. Though WTO is supposed to allow free trade for all, in practice, it is seen that the developed countries have unfairly retained trade barriers.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> Why do developing nations like India argue that WTO rules are biased in favor of developed countries?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypocrisy on Farm Subsidies:</strong> While the WTO forces developing countries to stop agricultural subsidies and open their markets to foreign food grains, developed countries (US and EU) continue to give massive government subsidies (billions of dollars) to their own farmers, depressing world prices and ruining third-world farmers.</li><li><strong>Unequal Bargaining Power:</strong> Developed nations use non-tariff barriers (sanitary standards, carbon border adjustments) to block goods from developing nations while forcing complete market access for Western goods.</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> Special Economic Zones (SEZs) have been set up in India to attract foreign companies to invest.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> Companies that set up production units in SEZs do not have to pay taxes for an initial period of five years and are provided with world-class infrastructure and flexible labour laws.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> To attract Foreign Direct Investment (FDI), state and central governments established SEZs equipped with uninterrupted electricity, water, roads, storage, and tax holidays for 5 years, along with relaxed labour hiring regulations.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(20)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(22)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "22": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch22\" data-book=\"economics\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">22</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Economics: Understanding Economic Development</div>\n      <h2>Consumer Rights</h2>\n      <p>Unit 22: Economics • Consumer Movement, COPRA Redressal Machinery, Rights & Standards — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text & Exercise Questions — Board Examination Solutions</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s22q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s22q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the factors which gave birth to the consumer movement in India? Trace its historical evolution.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The consumer movement arose in India as a 'social force' out of deep dissatisfaction of consumers against widespread exploitation by unscrupulous traders and manufacturers:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Rampant Unfair Trade Practices:</strong> Traders indulged in rampant malpractices like adulteration of food and edible oil, underweight measuring, and hoarding of essential goods to create artificial scarcity and hike prices.</li>\n  <li><strong>False and Misleading Advertisements:</strong> Companies made exaggerated and deceptive claims about products (medicines, baby milk powder, cosmetics) through mass media, duping uneducated consumers without any legal liability.</li>\n  <li><strong>Absence of Legal Framework:</strong> In the 1960s, there was no legal system available to consumers to protect them from exploitation in the marketplace. The traditional market rule was <em>caveat emptor</em> ('let the buyer beware'), placing the entire burden of substandard goods onto the consumer.</li>\n  <li><strong>Consumer Protests and COPRA Enactment:</strong> In the 1970s, consumer organizations formed consumer groups, published articles, held exhibitions, and staged rallies against adulteration. A major concerted campaign led the Indian Parliament to enact the historic <strong>Consumer Protection Act (COPRA) in 1986</strong>, providing consumers with statutory legal rights and dedicated consumer courts.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Exploitative trade practices: Adulteration, hoarding, false weights</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Deceptive advertisements and corporate lack of accountability</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Failure of traditional 'caveat emptor' market rules</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Evolution of organized consumer protests leading to COPRA 1986</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s22q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s22q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Mention a few rights of consumers and explain the significance of each under the Consumer Protection Act.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Under the Consumer Protection Act, consumers enjoy six fundamental statutory rights:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Right to Safety:</strong> Consumers have the right to be protected against the marketing of goods and delivery of services that are hazardous to life and property. For example, pressure cookers must have safety valves; substandard electrical appliances can cause fatal electric shocks or fire.</li>\n  <li><strong>Right to be Informed:</strong> Consumers have the right to be informed about the particulars of goods and services that they purchase — including ingredients, manufacturing date, expiry date, batch number, MRP, and directions for safe use. Consumers can also use <strong>Right to Information (RTI Act 2005)</strong> to know about government public services.</li>\n  <li><strong>Right to Choose:</strong> Any consumer who receives a service in whatever capacity, regardless of age, gender, and nature of service, has the right to choose whether to continue to receive the service. A gas distributor cannot force a consumer to buy a gas stove as a condition to grant a new cylinder connection.</li>\n  <li><strong>Right to Seek Redressal:</strong> Consumers have the right to seek redressal against unfair trade practices or exploitation. If any damage is done to a consumer, they have the right to get compensation depending on the degree of damage (refund, replacement, or financial damages).</li>\n  <li><strong>Right to Represent (Right to be Heard):</strong> Consumers have the right to represent themselves and be heard in consumer forums and judicial bodies.</li>\n  <li><strong>Right to Consumer Education:</strong> The right to acquire knowledge and skill to be an informed consumer throughout life (propagated via government awareness campaigns like <em>'Jago Grahak Jago'</em>).</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Right to Safety with practical examples (pressure cooker valve)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Right to be Informed (ingredients, expiry, RTI Act 2005)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Right to Choose (preventing forced tie-in sales)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Right to Seek Redressal (compensation, refund, replacement)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Right to Represent & Right to Consumer Education (Jago Grahak Jago)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s22q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s22q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the three-tier quasi-judicial machinery set up under the Consumer Protection Act for the redressal of consumer disputes.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Under COPRA, a <strong>three-tier quasi-judicial machinery</strong> at the district, state, and national levels was established for redressal of consumer disputes:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;margin-top:8px;\">\n  <tr style=\"background:#f0fdfa;color:#115e59;\">\n    <th>Judicial Tier</th>\n    <th>Pecuniary Jurisdiction (Claim Value)</th>\n    <th>Appellate Authority</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>District Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission (District Commission)</strong></td>\n    <td>Deals with consumer claims and compensation claims up to <strong>₹1 Crore</strong> (updated under CPA 2019). Located in every district headquarters.</td>\n    <td>If a consumer is not satisfied with the District Commission's order, they can appeal to the State Commission within 45 days.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>State Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission (State Commission)</strong></td>\n    <td>Entertains consumer claims exceeding <strong>₹1 Crore and up to ₹10 Crore</strong>. Located at the state capital.</td>\n    <td>Appeals against State Commission orders can be filed before the National Commission within 30 days.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td><strong>National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission (NCDRC)</strong></td>\n    <td>Deals with claims exceeding <strong>₹10 Crore</strong>. Located in New Delhi.</td>\n    <td>Appeals against the National Commission can be taken directly to the <strong>Supreme Court of India</strong>.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Three-tier quasi-judicial structure identification</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">District Commission jurisdiction and operational role</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">State Commission jurisdiction and appellate procedure</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">National Commission jurisdiction and Supreme Court appeal route</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s22q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s22q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the significance of quality certification logos: ISI, AGMARK, and HALLMARK. Why should consumers look for these marks?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Standardisation logos and marks help consumers ascertain that products meet prescribed safety, health, and quality standards established by government certification bodies:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>ISI Mark:</strong> Issued by the <strong>Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS)</strong>. It is mandatory for industrial and consumer safety products such as electrical appliances (heaters, irons), LPG cylinders, cement, packaged drinking water, and infant milk food. It guarantees that the product has undergone strict laboratory testing.</li>\n  <li><strong>AGMARK:</strong> Certification mark for <strong>agricultural products</strong> in India, issued by the Directorate of Marketing and Inspection. Found on pulses, cereals, spices, edible oils, ghee, and honey, certifying purity and absence of adulteration.</li>\n  <li><strong>HALLMARK (BIS Hallmark):</strong> Official certification mark guaranteeing the purity of <strong>gold and silver jewellery</strong>. It displays the BIS logo, purity grade in carats/fineness (e.g., 916 for 22-carat gold), assaying centre's identification mark, and a unique 6-digit HUID code.</li>\n  <li><strong>FSSAI Logo:</strong> Issued by the Food Safety and Standards Authority of India. Mandatory on all packaged food items, certifying that food products meet food safety and hygienic processing standards.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">ISI Mark: Electrical/industrial safety standards by BIS</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">AGMARK: Agricultural purity certification (pulses, oil, spices)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">BIS Hallmark: Gold and precious metal purity verification</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.25 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">FSSAI logo: Packaged food safety and hygienic handling</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case-Based Questions — Unit 22</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0d9488;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0d9488;\">Competency Case-Based Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Read the following extract and answer the question:</strong><br><em>'Reji Mathew, a healthy student, joined a private coaching institute for a two-year professional course and paid the entire fee of ₹61,000 for two years at the time of admission. However, after one year, he decided to leave the course because he found the teaching standard inadequate. When he asked for a refund of ₹30,500 for the second year, the institute refused. He filed a case in the District Consumer Forum.'</em><br><br><strong>Question:</strong> Which consumer right did Reji Mathew exercise, and what is the consumer court's legal verdict in such educational fee disputes?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Right Exercised:</strong> <strong>Right to Choose</strong> and <strong>Right to Seek Redressal</strong>.</li><li><strong>Consumer Court Verdict:</strong> The State Commission and National Commission have repeatedly ruled that coaching institutes and colleges cannot collect fees for the entire course in advance. If a student leaves after one year, charging fees for services not rendered is an unfair trade practice; the institute must refund the proportionate fee for the remaining period with interest and litigation costs.</li></ul></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #ea580c;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#ea580c;\">Assertion & Reason Assessment</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> The Consumer Protection Act 2019 replaced the older COPRA 1986 to address modern challenges.<br><strong>Reason (R):</strong> The 2019 Act covers e-commerce platforms, provides for product liability, established the Central Consumer Protection Authority (CCPA), and introduced mediation as an alternate dispute resolution mechanism.<br><br>Evaluate whether Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are correct and if (R) explains (A).</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>CBSE Competency Answer:</strong></p><p><strong>Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</strong></p><p><em>Explanation:</em> With the massive boom in online shopping, digital fraud, and deceptive teleshopping ads, the 1986 law was inadequate. The 2019 Act modernized consumer law by introducing strict penalties for misleading advertisements, holding e-commerce platforms liable, and enabling online filing of complaints (e-daakhil).</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(21)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <div></div>\n  </div>\n</section>\n"
};
